From 4c7cc178a87eb5cca431fd75e42611cfc9a8e806 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <claude@local>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 15:18:26 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 055/547] Add a 12-band parametric equalizer

A cascaded-biquad IIR filter bank (Robert Bristow-Johnson cookbook
coefficients, pure Kotlin - no native library, negligible size cost),
inserted into the existing custom DefaultAudioProcessorChain in
MusicService.createRenderersFactory alongside the silence-skipping
and speed processors already there.

- audio/Biquad.kt: per-band coefficient calculation (peaking/low
  shelf/high shelf/low pass/high pass) and the per-channel delay-line
  state each band runs through.
- audio/EqualizerAudioProcessor.kt: the AudioProcessor itself.
  Settings publish as a single volatile coefficients array so the
  playback thread never sees a half-updated set; disabled bands (or a
  fully flat/disabled EQ) take an identity-coefficient fast path that
  just copies the buffer through, so leaving it off costs nothing
  beyond the copy.
- models/EqualizerSettings.kt: schema'd, defensively-parsed JSON
  (same org.json approach PlayerLayout already uses), persisted to
  DataStore and reloaded on service start.
- ui/player/EqualizerSheet.kt: a Material 3 ModalBottomSheet - master
  switch, four built-in presets, a graphic-EQ row of vertical gain
  sliders (ui/component/VerticalSlider.kt - Compose has no vertical
  slider, so this rotates a horizontal one with a measure/layout swap
  rather than reimplementing drag handling), and a tap-to-expand
  parametric editor per band (log-scaled frequency, Q, filter type).
  Reuses the existing PlayerSliderTrack/SliderStyle system for the
  track's look rather than inventing a second one. Opened from the
  player's 3-dot menu, replacing the old system-EQ-panel shortcut.

Deliberately out of scope for this pass: multiple saved custom
presets (the four built-in ones cover the common cases for now), and
the shared PCM tap the audio-reactive visualizer will want - that
lands with the visualizer itself so the two don't develop competing
ideas about how the tap should work.
