From fcee136e31d0df59411f7e564f9ae652bddd1a81 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <claude@local>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 14:32:14 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 098/547] Recover unparseable lyrics on display, refetch once
 if they still fail

PlayerConnection.resolveLyrics (the actual live display path) called
the strict-only LrcUtils.parseLyrics directly on the stored row,
bypassing LyricsHelper's parseResilient() entirely - the lenient TTML
recovery that a fetch already uses to decide a result is usable in the
first place (classifyFound, in getRemoteLyrics). So content a fetch
had just classified as WORD_SYNCED or SYNCED could still show "Unable
to parse lyrics" the instant it was actually displayed: the fetch and
the display were reading the identical string with two different
levels of leniency, and only the fetch's was forgiving enough.

resolveForDisplay() (LyricsHelper) is the new entry point: try
parseResilient() first, same as a fetch would. Only when even that
can't read it does it fall through to a real failure - and now, rather
than leaving that as a dead end, it kicks off exactly one automatic
forceRefresh fetch for that song before returning the error
placeholder. autoRefetchedOnParseFailure caps this at one attempt per
videoId per process lifetime, so a provider that keeps returning
content nothing can parse gets retried once, not on every
recomposition. The refetch's DB write (if any) reaches the screen
through the existing reactive currentLyrics flow, not through this
call's return value.
