From 56805490d6d47176b1ef334114b63aeeb5ee3a2f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <claude@local>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 10:41:18 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 101/547] Two more defenses against error 2004 (bad HTTP status
 from googlevideo)

Beyond the cipher-config refresh already in place:

1. Retry once with a freshly-resolved URL instead of failing outright.
   A bad HTTP status (2004, almost always a 403) is frequently just a
   stale or otherwise-rejected stream URL, not a genuinely unplayable
   video - re-resolving from scratch, possibly landing on a different
   fallback client than the one that just failed, can well succeed
   where the cached URL didn't. onPlayerError now drops the cached URL
   for the failed song and re-prepares once
   (retriedAfterSourceError caps this at one attempt per song per
   process lifetime, so an actually-broken video still falls through
   to the normal skip/stop handling instead of retrying forever).
   songUrlCache moved from a local inside createDataSourceFactory() to
   a class field so onPlayerError can reach it to invalidate an entry.

2. Match the User-Agent to whichever client actually resolved the
   stream URL. YTPlayerUtils tries MAIN_CLIENT then STREAM_FALLBACK_
   CLIENTS in turn, each with its own real User-Agent (from
   YouTubeClient.userAgent) - but the actual media fetch, and the
   validateStatus() pre-flight HEAD check, both used a bare OkHttpClient
   with no header at all, so googlevideo.com saw a UA that didn't match
   the client the URL was signed for. PlaybackData now carries
   streamUserAgent (whichever client's stream actually got used), which
   flows through to validateStatus()'s HEAD probe and to the real
   fetch's DataSpec.withRequestHeaders() in both MusicService and
   DownloadUtil - the same fix in both places, since downloads hit the
   exact same CDN behaviour.
