From efea823e40b26a18cbc09bb84ae38ce6cb09b893 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <claude@local>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 11:58:18 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 107/547] Add opt-in AGSL GPU shaders: chromatic shock ripple
 and raymarched Ferrofluid

The lightweight Canvas Liquid renderer can't do real refraction or a lit
3D surface - both need a per-pixel shader, not a 2D draw call. AGSL
(RuntimeShader) gets that without pulling in a WebGL/Three.js-style engine:
it's a framework API on Android 13+, so the cost is just the shader source
text, not a bundled library.

Chromatic shock wraps the existing Liquid content and, on a beat, launches
a radial band that samples each colour channel at a different offset along
its own direction from centre - a refraction-driven chromatic split rather
than a flat overlay, narrowing as it travels outward. On by default
whenever Liquid is active; falls back to plain content below API 33 or if
the shader fails to compile on a given device.

GPU ferrofluid is a separate, heavier alternative to the Canvas Ferrofluid
shape: several metaball spikes raymarched and smooth-min blended into one
crown SDF, lit with a real per-pixel surface normal instead of a 2D
gradient standing in for one. Deliberately kept opt-in and off by default,
alongside (not replacing) the lightweight version, so battery/thermal cost
can be compared directly on a real device - which the lightweight version
exists specifically to avoid needing.

Both are gated behind Build.VERSION_CODES.TIRAMISU with runCatching around
shader compilation, so a hand-written AGSL source failing on some device's
Skia build degrades silently instead of crashing the player.

