From 32f5122eff7fb01003b693b6b884133fb4cf63c2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <c@example.com>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 12:26:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 114/547] Keep every stream request under googlevideo's
 connection-duration cutoff

Confirmed independently of any auth/URL-freshness issue: temporarily
raising CHUNK_LENGTH far beyond what any real song needs still failed with
the same source error, at roughly the same wall-clock point regardless of
the extra bytes available - a single long-lived connection to googlevideo
gets cut at around a minute, full stop, no matter how it's authenticated
or how fresh the URL is.

The fresh-resolve path already bounded its own request to CHUNK_LENGTH,
but the cache-hit path - which serves every request after the first -
didn't bound anything at all, so playback settled into exactly the kind of
long-lived single connection the CDN kills once the first small chunk ran
out. Bounds it the same way the fresh-resolve path already does.

Also adds a 45s OkHttp callTimeout as a second, independent line of
defense: if the chunk-boundary continuation doesn't trigger the way
expected on some device/ExoPlayer version, this forces a reconnect anyway,
comfortably before the ~60s point where the CDN itself cuts the connection,
rather than depending on one single mechanism always working.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
