Subject: [PATCH 133/547] Mint PoTokens against visitorData, never dataSyncId,
 even when logged in

Every PoToken this app minted while signed in was tied to dataSyncId -
permanent and bound to the Google account itself, unlike visitorData
(rotatable, not account-tied). If BotGuard ever flagged anything
associated with that dataSyncId, every future PoToken inherited the
flag permanently: no client-side action, including the "Reset YouTube
session" action, could shed it, since that dataSyncId never changes for
as long as the account exists. This is exactly why the identical Google
account can play fine through a sibling YTM client while failing here -
confirmed directly in Metrolist's source, which always mints against
visitorData regardless of login state and never touches dataSyncId for
this purpose at all.

playerResponseForPlayback/playerResponseForMetadata now always use
visitorData for PoToken generation, and rotateSessionIdentity() (the
"Reset YouTube session" action) is no longer gated to signed-out
sessions, since visitorData is now exactly as relevant logged in as
out. dataSyncId is untouched everywhere else - the request body's own
context.user.onBehalfOfUser still needs it for library/history/personalized
results while logged in; only the PoToken's own session identifier changes.

