From 0081dde0ed8a86d152b88ca62e64fcdf38db0715 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <claude@local>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 13:53:50 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 167/547] Fix downloads truncating to ~15s: drop the
 CHUNK_LENGTH subrange bound

Verified against the actual vendored media3 source
(exoplayer/offline/{DownloadManager,ProgressiveDownloader,DefaultDownloaderFactory}.java).
DownloadManager wraps whatever DataSource.Factory it's given in another,
outer CacheDataSource automatically, and ProgressiveDownloader drives the
whole download as one CacheWriter.cache() call against a DataSpec whose
length is C.LENGTH_UNSET ("everything"). Unlike ExoPlayer's own
chunk-based playback loader - which is what the .subrange(...,
CHUNK_LENGTH) pattern in MusicService relies on, since it re-invokes the
resolver for each successive chunk - the download path has no such
re-invocation. Bounding the resolved DataSpec's length there doesn't
chunk the download into many requests; it just ends the download after
that length, silently truncating every file to 256KB (~15s of audio)
instead of erroring, which is why this looked like it worked (no error
surfaced) while actually corrupting every download made since the
previous fix landed.

The callTimeout(45s) added in that same fix is what actually protects
downloads from the connection-duration cutoff - media3's DownloadManager
retries a caught IOException (default 5x) and CacheDataSource resumes
from the already-cached position, so a timeout costs no progress. Most
downloads (a few MB) finish in well under 45s and never hit it at all.
Removes the subrange bound from both the cache-hit and fresh-resolve
branches; keeps the dropped range= query param, the callTimeout, and the
trust-window-capped expiry/proactive PoToken refresh, which remain
correct.

