From 464d0a6e953005198c4b517487d590de07d6435b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <claude@local>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 15:25:20 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 183/547] Rework the GPU ferrofluid: skip empty space, theme
 its colours, make it move

Three things, aimed at it being worth running on a desktop-class GPU (a WSA
build on a PC) without becoming unusable on a phone.

Performance. The scene sits entirely inside a known bounding sphere, so
instead of marching out from the camera and spending steps crossing vacuum,
the ray is intersected with that sphere in closed form and the march starts at
its surface - and a ray that misses the sphere returns after one quadratic
rather than walking the whole step budget out to MAX_DIST. Since the crown
occupies a minority of the screen, most pixels were previously paying nearly
the full budget to discover they hit nothing; now they cost a dot product.
The march also stops at the sphere's far side rather than MAX_DIST, because
past it there is provably nothing left to hit. This buys more than trimming
step or spike counts would, and unlike those it costs no fidelity at all.

Colour. Highlight, rim and base are uniforms fed from the Material 3 scheme
rather than hardcoded, so the crown belongs to whatever palette the app is
wearing. Base comes from surface so it stays near-black on purpose:
ferrofluid reads as black sold by its highlight, and tinting the fill would
lose the effect rather than theme it.

Motion. Spikes now orbit (with per-spike drift, so the arrangement never
settles into a fixed wheel) and breathe outward and back in together on a
slow cycle that bass pushes wider - the radius they sit at is itself moving,
so the shape travels rather than pulsing in place.

Quality is now a setting (Low/Medium/High) rather than one fixed render
scale, since the sensible pixel budget differs enormously between a phone on
battery and a desktop GPU, and no single value serves both. High renders at
native resolution and skips the scale-down layer round-trip entirely; Medium
is the previous behaviour, kept as the default so nothing changes without
being asked for. The step count varies per tier via an early break inside a
constant-bounded loop rather than a uniform loop bound - AGSL follows GLSL
ES 2.0 rules, where the bound has to be a constant expression, so a uniform
there would have failed to compile and silently fallen back to the Canvas
version.
