From 784098a5a277bf1f98af68f4e44564c9aac8dec8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <claude@local>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 16:04:30 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 188/547] Bind PoTokens to the identity of the client that
 presents them

A PoToken is only honoured when it is bound to the same identity as the
request carrying it: dataSyncId for a client that sends the account
cookie, visitorData for one that goes out anonymous. An earlier commit
here changed this to mint against visitorData unconditionally, which
left WEB_REMIX - which does send the cookie, and names dataSyncId as
onBehalfOfUser - presenting a token for an identity it wasn't using.

Nothing rejects that outright, which is why it survived so long: the
player request still returns OK with a stream url, and the 256KB range
probe used to validate that url still passes, so resolution looks
completely healthy in the log right down to "found working stream".
googlevideo only refuses once the initial buffer is exhausted - about a
minute into playback - and immediately for the range-heavy reads a
download does, which is also why every queued download was failing with
a 403 while resolution reported success for each one.

Binds per client instead of once per resolve, memoised per identity, so
a resolve that tries both an anonymous and a signed-in client asks the
generator for each identity exactly once. PoTokenGenerator now caches a
streaming token per identity rather than holding a single one, so those
two coexist without tearing down and rebuilding the WebView on every
client switch; it recreates only on expiry or explicit invalidation.

Also logs which identity each stream url's token was bound to, at warn:
a token bound to the wrong identity resolves and probes exactly like a
correct one and only fails a minute later, so that was the one thing
needed to tell them apart and it was invisible in a release logcat.
