From 018b4ffd0dfc7f870f6f8187edfadb3196760e79 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <claude@local>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 16:57:58 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 190/547] Swap the PoToken bindings into their correct roles

YouTube's current web PoToken enforcement is GVS-only, and the two
tokens are not interchangeable: the token on the stream url must be
bound to the video id, and the player request's token to the session
(visitorData). This had them exactly backwards - the session token was
being appended to the stream url and the video-bound one sent in the
player body.

A session-bound token on the stream url is, to googlevideo, no token at
all. It serves the initial buffer and then refuses with a 403 about a
minute in, which is why this presented as "playback dies at 1min" and
survived every attempt to fix it from the other end: resolution reports
success, the 256KB validation probe passes, and the log reads healthy
right up to the point the CDN stops answering. Downloads, which read
ranges immediately, got the 403 straight away instead.

Metrolist's PlaybackClientCatalog states the rule outright, including
that a video-bound GVS token must not be reused for the player request.
Checked against that rather than guessed at, which is what the previous
attempts here were.

Also reverts the dataSyncId binding introduced by the previous commit.
It was a wrong answer to a real question: the session token is bound to
visitorData regardless of login state (PoTokenBinding.VISITOR_DATA), and
the binding that was actually wrong was which token rode on which
request, not which identity they named.

And drops useWebPoTokens from VISIONOS. That was added on the theory
that a bot challenge exists to be satisfied by a PoToken, but the token
comes from the web BotGuard and enforcement is web-client-only, so
presenting one from a visionOS client was a mismatch rather than an
answer. Metrolist's VISIONOS carries no PoToken either.
