From 8620077b4b4b7dd91abbb6871ef621a4d02fe170 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <claude@local>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 17:18:36 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 191/547] Resolve streams through MetrolistGroup/innertubex,
 with Faraday cipher configs

Adds innertubex as a dependency and routes stream resolution through its
InnerTubeExtractor ahead of this app's own client loop, which stays as
the fallback.

The case for a second engine rather than more repair to the first: the
PoToken rules this code kept getting wrong by hand - which token binds
to the video, which to the session, which clients need one at all - are
encoded as data in innertubex's PlaybackClientCatalog and covered by its
tests. It also brings SABR support, which this app has none of and which
is not optional for much longer, since ANDROID_VR past 1.65 already
answers SABR-only and the reachable share of the fallback chain shrinks
on YouTube's schedule rather than ours.

Deliberately scoped to the player path. innertubex has no metadata layer
to adopt - its InnerTube exposes the same raw transport as this app's own
and nothing above it - so browse, search, library and artist pages stay
on the existing, years-proven code rather than being rewritten against a
six-day-old API for behaviour they already have.

Metadata still comes from MAIN_CLIENT, and only the stream is taken from
innertubex: audioConfig carries the loudness this app normalises with and
playbackTracking is what registers history, neither of which innertubex
reports, so the split keeps both features intact.

Cipher configs come from MetrolistGroup/faraday, which watches for
player.js rotations, derives sig/nClass/sts/aliases and validates each
against a real CDN range request before publishing. That is the part of
signature handling that otherwise rots on YouTube's schedule - a
rotation invalidates hand-written configs and leaves deobfuscation
guessing until someone patches it by hand, which the upstream "add
cipher configs for current YouTube players" commits were doing.

Both engines share one PoTokenGenerator. Each instance owns a WebView and
mints its own session token, so a second would waste that and reduce
rotateSessionIdentity() to a half-measure - invalidating one engine's
tokens while the other kept presenting the identity just rotated away
from.
