From e2f64b3a1d51b569522da53cc1e97be35b01ffe7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <claude@local>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 19:06:16 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 195/547] Bound stream requests only for the clients that
 require it

Two failures with one cause, both of them mine.

Playback died partway into the first minute because every media request
was bounded to a 256KB range regardless of client. A WEB_REMIX url is not
served that way: a few dozen small bounded ranges in and googlevideo
answers 403. innertubex encodes the rule per client
(requiresBoundedMediaRange / usesChunkedMediaRanges / mediaRangeChunkSize)
and the web clients are read straight through; only ANDROID_VR, IOS and
TVHTML5_SIMPLY want a bounded range. StreamRangePolicy mirrors that so
both stream engines here agree, and the two request sites consult it.

The tell was in the symptom all along: death at 47s on one track and 60s
on another. What was being exhausted was a count of requests, so the
wall-clock point moved with the bitrate. A timeout would have landed in
the same place every time, and it never did. The note left beside the
bounding recorded the other half of the giveaway - raising the chunk size
changed nothing - and still concluded the url was aging out.

That wrong conclusion produced the second failure. Trusting a url for
only 20s means re-resolving three times a minute per playing song, and
each resolve is a /player POST. The log shows the playing song resolved
five times over while a download queue resolved fifteen more, and Google
answered with its /sorry/ abuse page: an HTML 403 on the API itself,
which failed downloads and playback alike. The workaround for the
imagined problem manufactured a real and worse one. Now half an hour,
still well inside YouTube's own claimed validity.

CHUNK_LENGTH stays, but only as cache-lookup granularity, and its comment
no longer claims to bound anything.

Verified by assembling coreRelease: compile, duplicate-class check and R8
clean, stopping only at the release keystore.
