From 8534368f650e3b5fad1b44da9d9c31759fdd764f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <claude@local>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 15:50:55 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 206/547] Add an in-app updater, and let a build ship only the
 languages it needs

Two things, both about how this app gets onto the phone.

The updater reads the releases API, picks the newest asset that is both
newer than this build and actually installable on this device, downloads
it, and opens the system installer on it. It never installs anything
itself and could not: Android requires the user to have allowed "install
unknown apps" for this app, and then shows its own confirmation every
time. So updating is one tap, but it is always the user's tap.

Asset selection is on versionCode parsed from the filename the build
already produces (OuterTune-0.19-arm64-v8a-core-release-147.apk), not on
versionName - versionName is free text and two builds routinely share
one, while versionCode is what Android actually decides an upgrade on.
The asset also has to match this device's abi and this build's flavour,
since neither a foreign-abi nor a different-flavour apk can replace this
install.

REQUEST_INSTALL_PACKAGES is the one new permission. FileProvider was
already declared and provider_paths already exposes cache-path, so the
download goes to cacheDir and is handed over from there - a half-finished
download is then something the system can reclaim rather than a file that
sits in app storage forever.

Two failure modes are called out in the source because they are opaque
from the outside: a workflow artifact cannot be used (zipped, needs an
authenticated call), and every build must be signed with the same key or
Android refuses the update outright with a signature mismatch, whose only
way out is an uninstall that takes the library with it. The workflow's
new publish_release step warns when no KEYSTORE secret is set, since that
is exactly the case where the updater downloads fine and then fails at
the install.

Separately, androidResources.localeFilters is now driven by -Plocales,
wired to a "locales" workflow input defaulting to en. Left blank the
build keeps all 50 languages, which is right for a release anyone might
install; a build made for one person was carrying strings that person
cannot read. English is always kept whatever is asked for - it is the
base values/ resource, so dropping it would leave any string a filtered
language happens not to translate with nothing to fall back to.

Measured on coreArm64-v8aRelease, same variant both ways: the linked
resource package goes from 903,067 to 190,361 bytes, so 712,706 bytes off
an apk that currently installs at 8.86MB.

Verified by compiling coreRelease with and without -Plocales=en.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
