From bae3a335aeb884a0d9fd09d20909f74bdc5f56e6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <claude@local>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 16:11:30 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 208/547] Collapse a renamed artist's two credits, and give the
 updater its own screen

An artist that renames itself upstream ends up credited twice on one
song, once under each name, and neither credit is right on its own.
((G)I-DLE -> i-dle is the case this was written against.) YTM keeps
returning the old name against the real channel, so that credit links
correctly and reads wrongly; the new name arrives as a bare Run with no
navigationEndpoint and lands as a local placeholder, which reads
correctly and links nowhere, so it shows greyed out. The song displays
both, and tapping the wrong-looking one opens a page titled with the
right name - which is the confusing part.

The fix is not to pick one of them. It is to notice they are the same
artist: a placeholder whose name resolves to a channel already credited
on this song *is* that channel under its current name. So the placeholder
is dropped and its name is written onto the real credit - the one the tap
already went to. What is left is a single credit, named as the page it
opens.

Only attempted when a song holds both a real channel and a placeholder at
once, so the search it costs is paid on the rare shape that can actually
be a rebrand rather than on every song.

The updater moves out of About into its own Settings entry above it. It
checks on open, says either that the build is current or that a named
release is available, and installs on tap. Three options: check on open,
download as soon as one is found, and which flavour to pull - core or
full - since only a matching apk can replace this install, and someone on
core otherwise has to find and sideload one by hand to move.

There is deliberately no "install automatically" switch to pair with the
download one. Android requires the user to have allowed installing
unknown apps and then confirms every install itself, so the furthest
automatic can go is having the apk already downloaded when the user says
yes. A switch promising more would be lying.

UpdateAvailableKey is now written for the first time. The search bar and
the settings list have both been reading it since before this change, but
nothing ever set it, so those indicators could not light up.

