From 17826adea6f45287593d519d393cd0eb967e2001 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: claude <claude@local>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 09:32:11 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 211/547] Ask for the whole file by range so downloads stop
 being paced

Downloads worked but crawled, on songs that streamed instantly. The
difference is that googlevideo paces a plain progressive request at
roughly playback speed. That is exactly right for playback, which is why
streaming was never affected, and useless for a download: a three minute
song takes about three minutes to fetch.

Metrolist v13.6.3 asks for the bytes explicitly instead, appending
&range=0-<contentLength> to the download url, and they arrive as a bulk
transfer at whatever the connection allows. This does the same.

contentLength is taken from the format where the client supplied one. A
few clients do not, and without a length there is no range to ask for, so
that case pays a HEAD request rather than silently falling back to being
paced. If even that fails the download still proceeds, just slowly, and
says so in the log.

Also fixes the artist collapse added last commit, which could not have
worked. It resolved the placeholder's name through resolveArtistEntity,
which prefers an existing library row with that name - and in this exact
case that row is the placeholder being examined. So it handed back the
placeholder's own local id, which never matched a real channel, and the
collapse never fired. It now resolves through resolveArtist, which
reports the channel the name belongs to upstream regardless of what is
already stored, which is the only thing that can identify the pair.

Verified by compiling coreRelease.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
