From 762960725643e5f3a90d74cd71ce4da9d9b2f8b6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@l>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 22:08:14 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 234/547] Estimate word timings so karaoke works on ordinary
 line-synced lyrics

The karaoke sweep only ever ran on lyrics carrying real per-word
timestamps, which almost nothing has. Nearly everything available is
line-synced LRC, and for those the setting did nothing at all.

A line already knows when it starts and when it ends, so the sweep can be
spread across its words in proportion to how long each word is. Length is
the weight rather than a flat split per word, because it is the better
proxy for duration: "I" and "everything" do not take the same time to sing.

This is a guess and is labelled as one. A singer does not deliver a line at
a constant rate, and held notes, rests and pauses are exactly what this
cannot know about, so the highlight drifts within a line and snaps back at
the start of the next. What it does not do is accumulate: every line begins
and ends on real timestamps, and only the distribution between them is
invented. Off by default, and shown as beta.

Nothing in the renderer changed. It works entirely off each line's word
list, so this is a matter of supplying that list. Lines that already carry
real word timings are left untouched - a genuine sync must always win.

Word boundaries are derived from the running weight total rather than by
accumulating per-word durations, so integer division cannot let the last
word finish early. Two cases fall back to the plain unswept line rather
than a degraded sweep: a line with nothing but whitespace, and a line
longer than 30s - the LRC parser has no next line to end the last one
against and ends it at Long.MAX_VALUE, which would inch the highlight along
at a rate of roughly nothing.

Verified by compiling coreRelease.

