From 1408472a628992790c90c26f377346c87e9fa137 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@l>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 22:11:26 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 235/547] Clean up YouTube caption tracks being used as lyrics

Auto-generated captions are a transcript, not a lyric sheet, and they carry
three habits that read badly as lyrics: ">>" speaker-change markers, which
mean something in dialogue and nothing in a song; "[Music]" dropped
mid-sentence wherever the recogniser heard backing over the vocal; and
"[Singing]" tagged onto lines that, being song lyrics, are obviously sung.

All three are removed. The one case worth keeping is a line that is nothing
but a music annotation, since that is describing a real instrumental
passage - those become the universal placeholder, which is what every other
lyrics source already uses for the same thing. A bare "[Singing]" was only
labelling the obvious and leaves an empty line instead.

Only music and singing annotations are touched. Auto-captions also emit
"[Applause]" and "[Laughter]", and those describe something genuinely on
the recording rather than an artefact of transcribing it, so they stay.

Lines that empty out are kept as empty lines rather than dropped: their
timestamps are still real, and a gap where the singing stops is correct.

Scoped to the caption provider alone. Every other source is supplying
actual lyrics, where a bracketed annotation is intentional and none of this
should apply.

Verified by compiling coreRelease, and by checking the two regexes against
the caption shapes involved: inline and standalone [Music], [Music and
singing], [MUSIC PLAYING], standalone and inline [Singing], [Applause],
lines with no text after their timestamp, LRC metadata tags, and text with
no timestamp at all.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../dd3boh/outertune/lyrics/CaptionCleanup.kt | 72 +++++++++++++++++++
 .../lyrics/YouTubeSubtitleLyricsProvider.kt   |  4 +-
 2 files changed, 75 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)
