From 2238c86c5d87b5d6c593b5936036748cd4300d6b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@l>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 18:04:54 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 242/547] Run the artist repair on launch, unstack the
 ferrofluid settings, fix the handle

Three fixes.

The artist repair only ran at the end of the remote-songs sync, on the
reasoning that the sync is what writes the fault. True, but it left the
repair unreachable on any device where that sync does not run - auto-sync
switched off, or simply inside its cooldown - so a library still carrying
the damage stayed broken indefinitely and neither updating nor reinstalling
touched it. That is why one device shows the fix and another on the same
build does not. Tying a fix for existing data to the thing that produces
new data was the mistake; it now also runs once per launch. Safe
unconditionally: it asks YouTube nothing and only reads the songs whose
stored rows show the fault, so a healthy library costs two indexed queries.

The ferrofluid reactivity setting was drawn on top of the quality one.
AnimatedVisibility lays its content out in a Box, so the two preferences I
put inside a single block were stacked rather than listed. Each has its own
block now. Checked the rest of the fragment for the same shape; this was
the only one.

The queue handle sometimes rendered down level with the system buttons.
playerAwareWindowInsets is remembered on the bottom inset and whether the
player is dismissed, but its body reads showNavRail - which decides whether
a navigation bar's height is added to that inset, and flips whenever the
player is expanded in landscape. It was not a key, so the insets kept
whatever value they were last built with and could be a whole navigation
bar out of step with the screen. showNavRail, isLandscape and tabMode are
all read in there and are all keys now.

Verified by compiling coreRelease.

