From 8a38597b4fa9b514299ad0b3011c24d7a9e8b55e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@l>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 18:32:58 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 244/547] Add a desktop stack probe, and correct what it says
 about a Windows port

The interesting question about a Windows port was never "does Kotlin run on
Windows" but which parts of the playback path are genuinely tied to
Android, and that is a question about YouTube's behaviour rather than about
our code - so it has to be asked rather than reasoned out. This module asks
it. It is a probe, not the start of a desktop app.

Two things it establishes.

:innertube works unmodified off Android. It is already kotlin("jvm") with
no Android imports, and search returns results on a plain JVM, so the whole
API layer ports for free.

More importantly, the browser is not needed for streaming. An earlier spike
concluded that every client failed without a PoToken and that a desktop
build would therefore have to embed a browser engine to run BotGuard. That
was wrong, and this probe shows why: the spike never set YouTube.locale or
visitorData, both of which App.onCreate sets on Android. Without them
YouTube answers with a bare 400 INVALID_ARGUMENT and refuses players with
"Video unavailable", which reads exactly like a token refusal and is not
one. With them set, IOS, ANDROID_VR_NO_AUTH, ANDROID_VR_1_43_32 and
VISIONOS all return audio formats carrying direct URLs - no PoToken, no
signature cipher, no JavaScript.

So the probe sets both up front and reports on them, precisely so that this
mistake cannot be made again from its output. WEB_REMIX and
TVHTML5_SIMPLY_EMBEDDED_PLAYER are still refused, which is expected: those
are the clients that want a PoToken.

Verified by running it on Windows 11 against a real video.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
