From cadc981d5178cf2a7ae301b4d4a595eb66534a40 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@l>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 19:08:43 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 246/547] Find out what desktop playback actually needs, by
 trying to decode

Media3 is Android-only, so a desktop build has to rebuild playback, and the
choice is between pure Java - one jar, nothing to install - and a native
library like VLC or GStreamer, which plays anything but has to be bundled
per architecture. That choice decides how the app is packaged, so it is
worth settling by decoding a real file rather than by assuming.

The probe reports the formats on offer, then fetches itag 140 (AAC-LC in
MP4, 130kbps, the one format every working client hands over with a direct
URL) and tries to turn it into PCM.

The result is more useful than a yes or no. The download works, the
container parses, and the metadata is all correct - AAC, 44100Hz, stereo -
and then zero frames come out. Every format YouTube offers is now
fragmented MP4; there are no progressive formats left at all, which the
probe checks and reports. A fragmented file keeps its samples in moof boxes
rather than in the moov sample table an ordinary MP4 reader walks, so the
metadata parses and the audio is unreachable.

That is a container problem and not a codec one, which is the distinction
worth having: swapping AAC decoders would change nothing. Pure-Java
playback is still open - it needs a fragmented-MP4 demuxer feeding a
pure-Java AAC decoder - and the probe now says so precisely instead of
claiming a native library is required.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
