From 2615ffb6451d30c079cd5ac7d1d8bef035cb6c5b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@l>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 20:09:56 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 248/547] Take the desktop audio chain all the way to something
 you can listen to

The demux probe proved frames come out and that their size is arithmetically
right. Neither of those shows the output is the song rather than noise, and
neither shows this machine will accept it, so the chain was proven in the
middle and unproven at both ends.

Both ends are covered now. javax.sound is asked whether a line for the
decoded format is actually supported and then opened - it is part of the
JDK, but a format still has to be supported by an available mixer, so that
is a question rather than an assumption. The line is only opened, never
written to: a probe should not start making noise on someone's machine
without being asked.

The decoded PCM is also written out as a WAV, which is the part a person
can check. Frame counts cannot tell you the decoder produced music.

On Windows 11 the whole path now runs: YouTube API to stream URL to bytes
to demuxed AAC frames to PCM to a supported output line, entirely in Java
with nothing native anywhere in it.

