From db98c8e2ace0bad0daf46aa0595da031c72c7c3d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@l>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 20:19:20 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 249/547] Write the WAV in the byte order the decoder actually
 uses

The probe reported success on static. The decode was right the whole time -
jaad emits big-endian PCM, and the WAV header declared little-endian, so
the file described correct data incorrectly and played as noise.

What let that through is worth fixing as much as the bug. Frame counts and
byte totals were the only evidence, and they are blind to byte order: the
output was exactly the right size and exactly wrong, which is precisely
what they cannot distinguish. "400 x 1024 x 2 x 2 = 1638400, therefore
correct" was true arithmetic and a false conclusion.

So there is now a check that can tell audio from noise. Music is a
continuous waveform, so consecutive samples sit close together, while noise
jumps across the full range every sample - and the mean step between
samples separates them cleanly. Measured both ways on this stream:
little-endian 21721, big-endian 2129. An order of magnitude, in favour of
the byte order the decoder said it was using all along, which is also the
check confirming the demux and alignment are right rather than merely
plausible.

The output format is taken from the decoder rather than assumed, in the WAV
and in the line-support check alike.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
