From 705fb9530e71af38cc0a7387daba25e69f8b11a0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@l>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 20:30:03 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 250/547] Add a desktop client that searches and plays, as one
 vertical slice

The probes established that every layer under the UI works off Android:
the API module is already plain JVM, four player clients hand over a direct
stream URL with no browser involved, and the fragmented MP4 those URLs
serve demuxes and decodes to PCM in pure Java. This is the first thing
built on top of that - search YouTube Music, click a song, hear it.

Deliberately one thin path rather than a scaffold. The Android screens
cannot simply be recompiled here: they reach for Hilt, Room, DataStore and
MediaSession through composition locals, none of which exist off Android,
so porting the UI is really a matter of rebuilding that plumbing under the
same composables. Taking one path all the way to audio surfaces exactly
which pieces are needed instead of finding out after a week of scaffolding.

Two build decisions worth recording. The Compose Multiplatform Gradle
plugin is not used - its releases track a Kotlin version behind the one
this project builds with - so the artifacts are taken directly and the
compose compiler plugin :app already uses does the real work. The cost of
that is having to name the Skiko native renderer explicitly, which the
plugin would otherwise pick for the host; without it the window opens and
immediately dies on a missing skiko-windows-x64.dll.

In the player, nothing about the output format is assumed. Sample rate,
channel count and byte order all come from the decoder's own first frame,
because the last thing to get this wrong wrote a header claiming
little-endian over big-endian samples and played correct audio as static.
Frames are decoded as the audio line consumes them rather than up front; a
four minute track is about 42MB of PCM and there is no reason to hold it.

What is missing, so it is not mistaken for finished: no queue, no library,
no database, no seeking, no pause, and the song is fetched in full before
it starts, so a large track waits a second or two first.

Verified by running it on Windows 11.

