From 34711c0512a60235a6ad4ca96bbfca8557d0caed Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@l>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 21:00:16 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 251/547] Try more than one client, and say which failed and
 how

Two faults, both of them mine, and both hidden by bad error reporting.

visitorData was fetched with runBlocking during composition on the AWT
thread, and its failure was silent. A silent failure there does not look
like one: search keeps working, because search only needs the locale, while
every player request comes back refused. "I can find the track but it says
no audio format" was exactly that, and nothing on screen said the session
had never been established. It is acquired in a LaunchedEffect now, and the
window reports connecting, or the reason it could not.

"No playable audio format" was also covering four different problems - the
player request failing, YouTube refusing the client, a response carrying
only ciphered formats, and itag 140 genuinely being absent - with one
message and four different fixes. Each says what it is now, which is what
turned the next failure into something diagnosable rather than a guess.

That next failure was a 403 on fetching the stream, and it exposed the real
design fault: one client, no fallback. googlevideo hands over a well-formed
URL and then refuses to serve it, so a URL being issued says nothing about
whether it will be fetched, and which client a given track will serve
varies by track. The probes never caught this because they only ever ran
against one hardcoded video. Four clients are tried in order until one
serves bytes, which is what the Android side does for the same reason, and
every client's own reason is kept rather than only the last - a 403 from
one and a refusal from another are different problems.

Verified by running it on Windows 11.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
