From e6a3a8f33b56f66f9af000423675df6ce097af28 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@l>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 21:09:43 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 253/547] Hold the decoder when paused, instead of trusting the
 audio line to

Pausing deleted the song. Stopping the line was the whole of the pause, on
the assumption that a write would block once the stopped line's buffer
filled and the decode loop would throttle itself against it. That is not
what happens on Windows: the writes kept returning, the loop ran through
every remaining sample in silence, reached the end and ended the track. So
pressing pause played the rest of the song at speed to nobody and then
reported it finished.

The decode loop is now held by a flag it checks itself, which does not
depend on any particular implementation's write semantics. The line is
still stopped as well, but only so that what is already buffered goes quiet
at once rather than playing on for a second after the button is pressed -
the two halves do different jobs.

The flag is also cleared when a track starts and when playback stops, so a
new song can never inherit the last one's paused state.

Verified by compiling; the behaviour needs a listen.

