From 19f1990be0523480d6365d709e0f6f368c1d72e4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@l>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 21:16:17 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 254/547] Add a queue and a library that survives closing the
 app

The queue is kept apart from the player on purpose. The player knows how to
play one song and to say when that song ended; the rule for what follows
lives in PlayerQueue. That keeps the player testable by asking it to play
something, and keeps "what plays next" in one place rather than spread
through it.

Advancing is driven by the player's onFinished, which fires only when a
track reaches its own end. Stopping or replacing a track deliberately does
not advance: those are the user leaving the song, and moving the queue on
for them would be the opposite of what they asked. Reaching the end of the
queue stops rather than wraps, since wrapping unasked turns a finished
queue into an endless one.

Clicking a search result queues everything after it rather than just that
song, which is what makes a result list behave like an album instead of a
series of one-song sessions.

The library stores recently played and liked songs in a text file, and that
is a deliberate placeholder rather than an answer. Choosing the real engine
- Room's KMP support, SQLDelight, something else - has consequences for
migrations, threading and how much of the Android data layer can eventually
be shared, and it is not a decision worth making as a side effect of
wanting a recently-played list. The surface is narrow enough that swapping
the backing store touches one file. What it costs is stated in that file
rather than left to be discovered: no queries, no indices, no migrations,
and the whole library rewritten on every change - fine for hundreds of
songs, wrong for thousands, which is when the real engine has to be picked.

Re-playing a song moves it up the recent list rather than adding a second
entry; fifty plays is far less useful than fifty songs when one track has
been on repeat.

Verified by running it on Windows 11.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
