From fe22aeeab090bf0f128bc2d471a03fa577b1f747 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@l>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 21:24:49 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 256/547] Show playback position, and make the track seekable

A player with no idea how far through a song it is was the larger gap of
the two obvious ones, and it is also the prerequisite for seeking.

Position is read from the audio line's own frame counter rather than from
how much has been written to it. Writing runs ahead of playing by whatever
the line has buffered, so counting written frames would show a position
roughly a second into the future - close enough to look right and wrong
enough to make a seek bar lie.

Seeking is close to free, and that is worth recording because it is a
direct consequence of a decision that otherwise looks like a shortcut.
Fetching the whole song before playing it means every AAC frame is already
in memory, and AAC-LC frames are exactly 1024 samples each, so a target
time is an index rather than a new range request. The line is flushed on a
seek, or what was already buffered would be heard first and the jump would
land a second late.

So the full-fetch design has two upsides, not just the cost of a wait: no
network remains to fail once audio starts, and seeking is arithmetic.
Streaming playback would remove the wait and give up both. The class doc
says so, so that trade is visible when someone reaches for streaming.

Verified by running it on Windows 11.

