From c0199d07175e203128597669c2ba6dc7dcc8dd09 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@l>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 21:29:09 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 257/547] Show album art, and make the app's size a thing you
 can ask rather than guess

Covers are cached per URL, which is less an optimisation than a
correctness matter for a lazy list: rows recycle constantly, so without a
cache every scroll re-requests the same handful of images and the list
flickers as they reload. The tile always occupies its space whether or not
an image has arrived, because a cover that appears only once loaded reflows
the row it lands in and makes the list jitter while scrolling.

The cache is bounded and clears wholesale at its cap rather than evicting
cleverly - the cost of being wrong is one reload of a visible image, which
is not worth an LRU.

The library stores a cover URL now too, so recently played rows have art
after a restart. Reading tolerates the three-field lines written before
this, so an existing library is not discarded by the change.

Also adds a sizeReport task, because "how big will this be" deserves a
measurement. It comes to 42.5MB of jars, and the largest single line is
Skiko at 9.6MB - Compose Desktop's native renderer, which is per-platform,
so a Windows build carries only the Windows one. That is the floor while
the UI is Compose, and the rest is ordinary library weight that shrinks
under minification. It is not the 100MB+ figure a bundled runtime produces,
and knowing which part is which is the point of being able to run the task.

