From afe842fbde72d1a1d154228cbc0c1b06a212f88d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@l>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 21:58:51 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 263/547] Use OuterTune's own icons, and add a fat jar task

The transport controls were emoji glyphs, which is not what OuterTune's
buttons look like. OuterTuneIcons holds the app's own vector paths, lifted
verbatim from the Android module's res/drawable vectors, so these are the
same shapes the phone draws rather than an icon set that resembles them.

material-icons-extended was the obvious route and was tried and dropped:
around 11MB for a handful of glyphs, against a 42MB total, to get icons
that would have been less correct than the ones already in the repository.
Android's XML vector format cannot be read off Android, but the only part
that carries meaning is the path, and PathParser reads exactly that - so
this costs nothing and matches exactly.

Play/pause is the one filled control, since it is the button being reached
for. Shuffle and repeat dim rather than disappear when off: a control that
vanishes is harder to find again than one that is plainly inactive.

Also adds a fatJar task, so the app can be handed to someone as one file
that needs only a JRE. Dependency signature files are excluded - several
ship signed, and a merged jar carrying their signatures fails verification
at startup with an error that says nothing about why. The jar itself is not
committed: it is build output that changes every commit and git would keep
every version of it forever.

Verified by running the jar standalone on Windows 11.

