From f8e2b26551a59e5fffbc311979a8b2079b108987 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@l>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 18:34:42 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 266/547] Stop bulk playlist adds being cancelled halfway to
 YouTube

Adding a lot of songs to a synced playlist added only some of them, and the
cause is a lifetime rather than an API limit. The loop posting each song to
YouTube ran on rememberCoroutineScope - tied to the dialog's composition -
and the dialog calls onDismiss() as soon as the local rows are written. The
scope is cancelled the moment it leaves the tree, so the loop was racing
that cancellation: one song usually won, a hundred did not. The database
always had them all, which is why it looked like a sync fault rather than a
cancelled coroutine.

It now runs on SyncUtils' own scope, which is application-scoped and
outlives any screen. Sequential and paced at 60ms rather than fired at
once: each add is a separate write to the same playlist, and a hundred
simultaneous writes are a good way to be rate-limited and a good way to
have them land out of order.

Failures are counted and logged rather than discarded. Every call returned
a Result that nothing looked at, so a playlist YouTube refused outright was
indistinguishable from one that worked.

Found while fixing that: both duplicate-dialog paths - "skip duplicates"
and "add anyway" - wrote the local rows and never contacted YouTube at all.
Not a race, simply missing, so those adds never synced no matter how few
songs were involved. All three paths now go through one helper, so a caller
cannot do the local half and forget the remote half.

Verified by compiling coreRelease.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
