From 6dd561e031a97936c359691f8be7f5a85d99f9e9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@l>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 21:43:44 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 268/547] Put the action row where the app actually puts it,
 and pin the queue handle

"What are sleep, like and the three dots doing down there" was a fair
question with a real answer: nothing, they do not belong there. The app
draws that cluster inline with the title - Player.kt's own comment at the
call site reads "action buttons for portrait (inline with title)" - and the
editor had been placing it in a strip below the transport row, which is not
a layout the app ever draws. Every preset now puts it directly after the
title, so a preset describes the player rather than inventing one.

The queue handle could be pushed off the bottom of the screen. It was a
flex item with margin-top:auto, which puts it at the bottom only while
everything above happens to fit; a large cover on a shorter screen pushed
the column past the surface and took the handle with it. Reproduced at
117px off-screen with the artwork preset on a 1600px-tall screen. It is
positioned against the bottom edge now, which is what it is in the app - the
queue sheet's peek, always there regardless of what is above it.

Added a warning for the underlying condition rather than only its symptom:
the surface clips, so an over-full stacked layout silently lost its lowest
blocks and the preview looked fine when the phone would not. It now says
how many pixels too tall it is.

Three new presets, shaped after players that have already answered these
questions. Airy keeps a small cover high with the transport low and a lot of
air between. Cinema leans on a very large cover with tight controls beneath.
Everything is the dense end with no hidden controls - and the only preset
that turns the seek buttons on, since that is its whole point.

Verified in a browser across all seven presets: queue overflow 0px
everywhere it is visible, zero overlapping pairs in free placement, and the
too-tall warning firing when a layout genuinely does not fit.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
