From 4964055b81aa72f8c406fdafe2bd0794cec594db Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@l>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 21:55:46 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 270/547] Add alignment guides, keep blocks inside the player,
 and space the controls properly

Alignment guides, PowerPoint-style. A line appears while a dragged block
lines up with the player's centre or with another block's centre, and the
drag snaps onto it. The centre line is solid and the block-to-block ones
dashed, so "centred on the screen" is distinguishable at a glance from
"lined up with that other thing" - previously the only feedback was an
outline colour, which said a block was aligned without saying to what.

Blocks are now bounded by their edges rather than their centres. Clamping
the centre to 0..100 let half of a block sit outside the player area, which
for the queue handle meant it could end up half under the navigation bar -
exactly where it must not be. Bounding by the half-extent stops a block when
its edge reaches the edge.

The control row was crowded, and the cause was a fixed 14px gap chosen when
every control was drawn at the same nominal size. Once play/pause became
genuinely larger than its neighbours that stopped being enough: a 52px
circle with 14px either side reads as cramped beside 30px glyphs, because
the eye judges a gap against the size of what it separates rather than in
absolute pixels. The gap is a fraction of the row's largest control now, in
stacked mode and in the seeded free-placement row alike, so spacing looks
constant at any scale. Measured: 22px at 100%, 28px at 130%, 15px at 70%.

Verified in a browser: gaps scale with the controls, free placement still
seeds with zero overlapping pairs, and no guide elements are left behind
when a drag ends.

