From 75ba129791d82c23d39e9042ac8980ebad0c1b46 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@l>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 12:14:02 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 274/547] Add the listen-together core: wire format, clock sync
 and position anchoring

The three pieces a sync feature is actually built on, written first and on
their own because they are the only parts that can be tested cheaply - all
three are pure Kotlin with no Android and no clock of their own, which is
what makes 27 unit tests possible before a single socket exists.

Protocol is hand-rolled and fixed-width rather than JSON. A tick is 26
bytes against roughly 120 as JSON, and it is sent every second for the
length of a session, so the format is paid for continuously. Every frame
carries the whole state rather than a delta: a follower that misses one is
corrected by the next instead of being permanently wrong, which removes
resynchronisation, sequence gaps and persistent divergence as concerns
outright. Times are the monotonic clock in microseconds, never wall clock -
an NTP correction mid-session would look exactly like a huge sync error and
provoke a seek.

ClockSync is NTP's four-timestamp estimator, because the problem is NTP's.
The detail that matters is taking the minimum-delay sample rather than the
average: path asymmetry is the only error the formula cannot see through
and it grows with delay, so the quickest exchange is the least contaminated
measurement available. Tested against known offsets under simulated
conditions, which is the only honest way - with two real clocks the true
answer is precisely what is unknown. It recovers a symmetric path exactly,
and errs by half the asymmetry and no more on a lopsided one, which is the
theoretical floor.

PositionAnchor exists because player.currentPosition only moves every few
hundred milliseconds, and a staircase on both ends of a sync link is the
largest error in the whole feature - the two do not cancel, and together
they are enough to trip a correction that was not needed. It latches the
last real movement and interpolates, so error stays inside one player tick;
measured at 5ms worst case over a simulated minute.

One real bug found by writing the tests rather than after: a Float rate is
not the decimal it looks like, so 1.02f times a second of playback comes to
1019.99998ms and truncating discarded a millisecond every second. Rounded
now.

Verified: 27 tests, all passing.

