From c11a36c34fadf04c5ca0b073688ac3136b7ef59d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@l>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 09:49:52 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 275/547] Listen together: transport, discovery and drift
 correction

Builds the second layer on top of the tested core (Protocol/ClockSync/
PositionAnchor).

LanTransport - TCP over the local network. TCP rather than UDP because
these frames are rare and small, so head-of-line blocking has nothing to
block, while ordering and detectable disconnection come free. Framing
uses readFully throughout: a bare read() returns short whenever a frame
straddles two TCP segments, which would desynchronise the stream
permanently and silently. TCP_NODELAY is set, since Nagle would hold
back exactly the small frames whose timing is the point.

Deliberately free of Android imports, so it is tested against real
loopback sockets on a desktop JVM rather than only on two phones.

LanDiscovery - DNS-SD via NsdManager. Browses for a service type rather
than scanning the subnet, so "is OuterTune installed" is answered by the
device appearing in the list at all. Resolution is serialised through a
queue: before API 34 the platform permits one resolve in flight, and the
natural code resolves each service as it is found, which silently loses
every host after the first.

DriftCorrector - graded corrections. Under 30ms nothing happens, since
the precedence effect hears two sources that close as one. Up to 250ms
the rate is nudged a few percent, which Media3 time-stretches so pitch
is unchanged and nothing is audible. Beyond that it seeks, because the
gap is already an obvious echo. Hysteresis stops a boundary gap
flapping, and a settle window after a seek breaks the seek loop that
follows from trusting a player's position mid-seek.

47 tests pass, including a clock sync exchange over real sockets and a
simulated 200ms gap closing with no seek and peak rate 1.05.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
