From 63e2376b97b798a7c2ad0b833cb386094ab8d7f9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@l>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 09:59:36 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 276/547] Listen together: host and follower sessions

PlaybackBridge is the seam. Both sessions hold the timing logic and
neither touches Media3, Hilt or a Context, so they can be driven by a
fake player at any speed with a scripted network on the other side.

HostSession is deliberately passive about followers: it never waits for
one, slows for one, or is seeked by one. The host's experience is
identical whether anyone is listening or not, which removes every
failure mode where one bad connection degrades everyone else. It polls
the player at 250ms but broadcasts at 1s, because the anchor needs
readings at least as often as the player moves to catch when it actually
advanced.

FollowerSession measures the clock offset first and rapidly, and
corrects nothing until it converges - a correction computed against an
unknown offset is a seek to an arbitrary position, which is visibly
worse than not syncing at all.

SyncMath is separated out and pure because it is the calculation the
whole feature rests on and a sign error in it looks exactly like a
working implementation until two devices are side by side.

The compiler caught a real design smell: `val isPlaying` alongside
`setPlaying()` clash on the JVM for any implementer using a var. Renamed
to setPlayWhenReady/setPlaybackSpeed, matching Media3, which also makes
the Android implementation a straight passthrough.

67 tests. The end-to-end one runs a real host and follower over a socket
with players as coarse as the real thing and lands them within 150ms.
It first failed at 60 seconds apart - a Long.MIN_VALUE sentinel in the
fake overflowed when subtracted from a clock reading, so the fake player
reported position zero for an entire session.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
