From 98de0c308a8c84d401a279e0162e46694e2c0c25 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@l>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 10:12:57 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 278/547] Listen together: fix three concurrency and lifecycle
 bugs

Found reviewing the integration layer, all three only reachable at
runtime.

Discovery mutated its host map from two threads: NSD delivers callbacks
on a binder thread while resolution completes on a coroutine, so a
device leaving the network just as another was being resolved would
throw ConcurrentModificationException out of the iteration that
publishes the list. Both kinds of change now go through the queue that
already existed for serialising resolves, and one coroutine owns the map.

The host tracked only peers that had completed a handshake, so a
connection that stalled mid-introduction - or a port scan, or an app
killed halfway through joining - kept its socket and coroutine forever.
Accepted links and greeted peers are now tracked separately: the first
for lifetime, the second for broadcasts and the listener list, so a
half-open connection is also no longer a phantom entry in the UI.

A cancelled session still runs its finally block, and could do so after
the next session had started - resetting the mode of the session that
replaced it. Sessions now carry a generation and late cleanup is ignored.

Also swapped a shadowed non-atomic StateFlow.update for the real one.

68 tests.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
