From 8645daa3a054f5195e0542bdf0fbcab3228231d9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@l>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 10:18:29 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 280/547] Listen together: stop a buffering follower restarting
 the song each tick

Handing a queue to the service returns long before the player reports
the new song - the queue still has to be built and the media item
prepared. So the next tick still saw the old track, asked for the same
song again, and restarted it from the host's position. Once a second,
for as long as buffering took, which is exactly the window in which the
user is watching and concluding the feature does not work.

A settle window now covers that gap, keyed on the video id so a host
that genuinely skips to another song is still followed at once rather
than ignored for the rest of the window.

Writing the test for it needed the fake player to lag the way a real one
does, and that immediately showed something worth keeping: a track
change landing mid-load is picked up by the following tick rather than
queued. That is the whole-state-every-frame design repairing itself, and
it is why there is no pending-change queue to get out of order.

71 tests.

