From 6586204f653fc2f22f725efad0880bf389a4b785 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@l>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 10:44:41 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 282/547] Listen together: six bugs from a code sweep

Protocol truncated strings by character while the length prefix and the
decoder's bound counted bytes. 512 characters of Japanese is 1536 bytes,
so a long CJK or emoji title either overflowed the encode buffer -
throwing out of encode and killing the connection - or failed the length
check on arrival and was dropped, leaving the follower permanently
unaware of what was playing. Truncation is now by byte, backing up over
UTF-8 continuation bytes so a character is never cut in half, and the
buffer size is derived from the limits rather than guessed. Two existing
tests covered multi-byte and over-long separately; neither covered both
at once, which is exactly where it broke.

A follower took ticks as authoritative even when the host had never
named a track - a host with an empty queue sends ticks and no TRACK at
all - and would seek the user's own unrelated song to a position that
meant nothing.

The host reported playWhenReady rather than whether audio was actually
advancing. While it rebuffered, followers kept projecting forward, ran
ahead by the length of the stall, and were then yanked back by a seek.
Media3's own isPlaying reports the truth, so both ends pause and resume
together.

A follower whose host switched to a local file carried on playing
whatever it had before, audibly out of step with the session it claimed
to be in.

Tapping start then immediately stop left the device advertised on the
network forever: stop ran while the advertisement was still null and the
bind completed afterwards. Advertising now hops to the main scope and
checks the session generation, so it cannot interleave with stop.

A failure to bind the listening socket surfaced as a failed flow, and
with nothing catching it that reached the default handler and would have
taken the app down.

Also hardened two paths: a frame that will not encode now costs one
frame rather than the connection, and an NSD resolve that never calls
back - which happens - times out instead of stalling discovery so that
the first host found is the only one ever shown.

74 tests.

