From 0b95bf1ccce5645b5e3d15d5d8efceaf603ed6a9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: test <t@t>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 15:49:33 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 287/547] Build workflows: stop the failure reporter from
 failing

This step runs only when something has already gone wrong, so a reporter
that dies takes the real cause off the screen and replaces it with its
own error. It did that in two ways, and between them it broke in exactly
the cases it exists for.

It grepped build.log unconditionally. That file is written by the build
step, so its absence means the build never started and the failure is
upstream - the patch, the SDK licences, the signing key. Reporting "No
such file or directory" presented the missing log as the problem, which
is the least useful thing it could have said. It now recognises that
case and names the likely step instead.

And the annotation loop failed whenever there was nothing to annotate.
Piping an empty string into `while read` still yields one blank line,
`[ -n "$line" ] && echo` returns 1 for it, that was the loop body's last
command, so the loop failed, so the pipeline failed, and under `set -e`
the step died. Which is to say: it worked when there were compiler
errors and fell over when there were not.

Both verified by running the old and new logic against three situations -
no build.log, a build that failed with no compiler errors, and a real
compile error. The old one exits 1 on the first two; the new one exits 0
on all three and still emits annotations for the third.

Also drops `set -e` from this step. A diagnostic that aborts on the first
non-zero return is a diagnostic that stops working the moment there is
nothing to find, and it appends the last 40 lines of the build when no
compiler error matched, so a Gradle or configuration failure is visible
rather than merely asserted to exist.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
