From 0362f55d12205fc76d2b6375a73889f8a6241456 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 15:58:48 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 288/547] Generate the patch with a script, so the exclusions
 cannot be forgotten

Two exclusions decide whether the patch applies on the runner, and both
were being retyped by hand.

*.patch, because the patch is tracked - the workflow reads it from the
repo root - so without it each regeneration embeds the previous copy of
itself. Measured once at 4.3MB becoming 8.7MB for two small commits, and
it doubles again every time after.

.github/workflows/*, because the workflows live on the branch the build
is dispatched from and are uploaded there directly. Shipping them
through the patch as well means the patch rewrites the workflow applying
it, and an edit on either side collides with the other. That is what
actually broke the last build: commit 284 failed on
build-new-patched.yml, because a line-ending normalisation had rewritten
the whole file on one side while dev had its own uploaded copy on the
other.

The cost is that workflow changes must now be uploaded to the build
branch by hand. That is smaller than the alternative.

The script also fails if a workflow diff leaks back in, so the mistake
cannot be made silently.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 tools/make-patch.sh | 56 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 56 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 tools/make-patch.sh

diff --git a/tools/make-patch.sh b/tools/make-patch.sh
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..d817e6a7b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/tools/make-patch.sh
@@ -0,0 +1,56 @@
+#!/usr/bin/env bash
+#
+# Regenerates outertune-metrolist-engine.patch.
+#
+# The exclusions are the whole point of having this as a script rather than a command someone
+# retypes, because getting either of them wrong produces a patch that looks fine and fails on the
+# runner.
+#
+#   ':(exclude)*.patch'
+#       The patch is tracked in the repo, because the build workflow reads it from the repo root.
+#       Without this, each regeneration embeds the previous copy of itself - once measured at 4.3MB
+#       becoming 8.7MB for two small commits, and it doubles every time after that.
+#
+#   ':(exclude).github/workflows/*'
+#       The workflows live on the branch the build is dispatched from, and are uploaded there
+#       directly. Shipping them through the patch as well means the patch rewrites the workflow
+#       that is applying it, and any edit made on either side collides with the other. That is not
+#       hypothetical: it is what made commit 284 fail to apply, on build-new-patched.yml, after a
+#       line-ending normalisation rewrote the whole file on one side.
+#
+#       Workflow changes therefore have to be uploaded to the build branch by hand. That is a real
+#       cost, and it is smaller than the alternative.
+#
+#   --base
+#       Records the commit this was built against, so the workflow can check the checkout is
+#       actually there before trying - and move if not. Without it, a base mismatch surfaces as a
+#       wall of rejected diff with no hint that the base is the problem.
+#
+set -euo pipefail
+
+BASE="${1:-93260340a4f9d8d275485b7784d317b875cb7d71}"
+OUT="${2:-outertune-metrolist-engine.patch}"
+
+cd "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)"
+
+if ! git cat-file -e "${BASE}^{commit}" 2>/dev/null; then
+  echo "base $BASE is not in this repository" >&2
+  exit 1
+fi
+
+git format-patch "${BASE}..HEAD" --stdout --base="$BASE" \
+  -- . ':(exclude)*.patch' ':(exclude).github/workflows/*' > "$OUT"
+
+commits="$(grep -c '^From ' "$OUT" || true)"
+bytes="$(wc -c < "$OUT")"
+echo "$OUT"
+echo "  base:    $BASE"
+echo "  commits: $commits"
+echo "  bytes:   $bytes"
+echo "  sha256:  $(sha256sum "$OUT" | cut -d' ' -f1)"
+
+# A patch that still carries a workflow diff would reintroduce the collision above.
+if grep -q '^diff --git a/\.github/workflows' "$OUT"; then
+  echo "ERROR: workflow diffs leaked into the patch" >&2
+  exit 1
+fi
-- 
2.45.1.windows.1


From 594f98b7c35aeaf6da1005349107a304183cf634 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 16:58:22 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 289/547] Desktop: a visualiser that matches what is being
 heard

The spectrum itself is ordinary DSP - Hann window, radix-2 FFT,
log-spaced bands, decibels - and all of it is tested against signals
whose answer is known in advance, because none of it can be checked by
looking. An FFT with a transposed index, a byte order taken the wrong
way round, or a missing sign extension all produce bars that move
plausibly in time with the music.

The part that is not ordinary is the alignment. Audio is written to the
output line well before it is heard, since the line buffers close to a
second - so a spectrum computed when a block is decoded describes music
that has not reached the speakers yet. Drawn directly, the bars lead the
sound by about a second: they jump before the kick and settle before the
note ends. That does not read as a decoration, it reads as a fault.

So each spectrum is tagged with the frame position at which it becomes
audible and held until the line reports having played that far. It is
the same lag DesktopPlayer already corrects for when reporting position,
by reading the line's own frame counter rather than counting what it has
written. A seek re-anchors both, because flushing the line discards
audio that was written but never played.

Bands are logarithmic and levels are in decibels because pitch and
loudness both are; linear bins put six of the seven audible octaves into
the top quarter of the display. Bars rise instantly and fall slowly,
since band energy is bursty and a bar that tracks it in both directions
only flickers.

Byte order is taken from the decoder every time rather than assumed -
jaad returns big-endian, which is the opposite of what most examples do,
and reading it wrongly produces plausible noise rather than an error.
That exact mistake already cost a session on this project.

27 tests. No new dependency: an FFT is fifty lines, and the jar is
unchanged at 44MB.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
