From 3e56e5177e0108bf3b5ca35bb4e192318dedacb9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 18:09:16 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 290/547] Listen together: stop the microstutter, and let the
 lag be dialled out

Two complaints, two different causes.

The stutter was the correction itself. Every change of playback rate
reconfigures the audio pipeline, and on many devices that is heard - so
refining the rate every tick as the gap shrank produced a string of small
artefacts, which is precisely what the rate correction exists to avoid.
Simulating the old logic, closing a 200ms gap took eight rate changes:
eight chances to be heard. A rate is now kept for three seconds before it
may be refined, which closes the same gap in three or fewer. Handing the
rate back is exempt from the dwell, since a follower that has caught up
must stop running fast immediately or it overshoots.

The lag is not something the app can measure. Sync is computed between
the two players' reported positions, but neither of those is what anyone
hears: each device puts sound out some time after its player says so, and
how long depends on the route - Bluetooth alone is a fifth of a second.
So two devices can agree exactly on position and still be audibly apart,
and nothing in the protocol can see it. There is now a timing offset in
the settings, applied live so it can be tuned by listening, which is the
only way to find the right value.

The same invisible offset also had the corrector seeking against a gap no
seek can close - an interruption every couple of seconds, forever. After
two attempts it now stops trying and leaves it to the rate, which at
least is inaudible.

Four new tests, including one that pins the number of rate changes,
because the version that stutters passes every other test in the file.

Also: a "view artist" tap no longer lands on an auto-generated "- Topic"
channel. YouTube attributes an upload to one of those alongside the
artist's real channel, so a song is routinely credited to both - and the
topic channel's page does not even carry the right name, while the song
sits under the real artist's singles. Credits that name nobody are now
set aside, which also means a song credited to one real artist and one
topic channel links straight through instead of asking which was meant.

And the artist header no longer fills a landscape screen. A full-width
square is right in portrait and far too much when width is the long edge:
opening an artist filled the display with one very bright picture and
nothing else.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
