From 4709bafe022b5104ab84bb500e838794b03e5565 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 20:59:25 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 292/547] Desktop: a software equaliser at the only point audio
 can be shaped

Twelve peaking biquads, applied to the decoded PCM in place immediately
before it is written to the output line. Not a design preference: Java
Sound has no effect chain, so between the decoder and the write is the
only opportunity there is. That also makes this a real implementation
rather than a binding to something the platform supplies, which is what
audiofx.Equalizer is on Android.

Applied before the visualiser measures the signal, which is the only
arrangement that is not confusing - boosting the bass should move the
bass bars.

Measured rather than listened to. A filter that is subtly wrong still
produces sound, and usually sound that is merely a bit off, which is
indistinguishable from the setting having worked. So every test puts a
tone of known frequency through and measures what came out in decibels:
+6dB asked for, +6dB measured, and a distant band that does not move.

Three things it would be easy to get wrong, each pinned:

Every channel needs its own filters. A biquad remembers two input and two
output samples, so one filter run across an interleaved stream feeds
left's history into right's output and back - a comb filter across the
stereo image, not a subtle degradation. The test feeds a tone to one
channel only and fails if the silent one comes out loud.

Byte order applies on the way out as well as in. The decoder returns
big-endian; reading one way and writing the other is not an error, it is
noise, and it would pass any test that only checked the samples changed.

Clipping must clamp, not wrap. An integer that overflows goes from
loudest positive to loudest negative, which is heard as a crack rather
than as distortion.

Band centres match the Android app's DEFAULT_FREQUENCIES exactly, so a
set of gains means the same thing on both and presets could move between
them. That is also the point of a test that pins the list.

The biquad here and the one in the Android module are now the same filter
written twice, including two pieces of hardening learned there. They
should share a module. HANDOVER.md says why they do not yet and what the
fix is.

40 desktop tests. The jar is unchanged at 44MB - no new dependency.

