From 69e065a52f5e04189b9906b906bad1d05fbe175b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 21:18:11 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 293/547] Desktop: SQLite storage, and playlists on top of it

The text-file store said the real engine had to be chosen at the point
where "the whole library is held in memory and rewritten on every change"
stopped being acceptable. Playlists are that point: a reorder rewriting
every playlist to disk is not a design anyone would choose on purpose.

SQLite through the JDBC driver, SQL written out rather than generated. No
Gradle plugin and no code generator, for the same reason this module
takes Compose as artifacts - both pin versions that drift against the
Kotlin this project builds with, and neither earns that for a schema this
size. SQLite specifically because the Android side already is, so a
schema and a query mean the same thing there.

The driver ships natives for eleven platforms; fatJar keeps only the
host's. +2.5MB rather than +14MB.

One connection behind one lock. Several connections mean a write in one
is invisible to a read already in flight in another, and the failure is a
lock timeout under exactly the conditions hardest to reproduce. The lock
is reentrant, so transaction counts its depth - a nested call joins the
outer one rather than committing it early, which is a bug easy to write
and hard to see.

Schema versioned with SQLite's own user_version rather than a migrations
table: already there, atomic with the transaction that sets it, and it
cannot itself need migrating.

An existing text-file library is imported once and the files renamed
rather than deleted, so a failed import can be looked at and a second run
cannot duplicate anything. Throwing away someone's liked songs because
the storage improved would be a poor trade.

Playlists: create, rename, delete, add, remove, reorder, all persisted.
Reordering deletes and re-inserts in one transaction rather than updating
positions one at a time, because the song is part of a unique constraint
and the position is not - moving one song would collide with whatever
holds its destination. Removal renumbers, because leaving a hole reads
fine in order and then quietly breaks the next insert.

63 desktop tests, including that opening an existing database twice is
not an error, that an upsert never blanks a cover it does not know about,
and that a failed reorder leaves the playlist exactly as it was.

Also, for the Android side: an artist page with no Songs section is now
treated as a candidate rather than an answer. YouTube gives one artist
several channels and they are not equivalent - one can carry only Singles
and EPs while another, named identically and equally real, carries the
full Songs listing including the song that was tapped. Landing on the
first is technically correct and useless. Bounded to two extra lookups.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
