From acc2f10534bd8cb4d23a7fe53307954bf8bcf47d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 09:22:51 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 295/547] =?UTF-8?q?=EF=BB=BFSign-in:=20state=20the=20actua?=
 =?UTF-8?q?l=20problem,=20from=20the=20code?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Rewritten against what InnerTube.kt actually does rather than from
recollection.

Authentication is SAPISIDHASH - Google's internal first-party scheme -
not OAuth. The credential is a logged-in web session, and only SAPISID
feeds the signature. That is why 'do the OAuth dance' has no answer here:
not because it is hard, but because an OAuth token authenticates the
YouTube Data API, which is a different service with no playback.

It also disposes of the embedded-small-browser idea as a category. The
cookie comes from accounts.google.com, whose sign-in is a JS application
behind BotGuard. NetSurf's JavaScript is a partial Duktape engine meant
for progressive enhancement, and the same is true of every lightweight
engine: anything that can pass Google's sign-in is approximately
Chromium, and anything smaller cannot.

What remains: importing a cookies.txt file (small, certain, teachable),
reading Firefox's unencrypted cookie store, TV device OAuth, or bundling
Chromium at ~150MB.

The device-OAuth option is written up as an open question rather than an
answer, with the specific thing worth checking spelled out - whether a
self-created 'TVs and Limited Input devices' client yields a token
InnerTube accepts with the TVHTML5 context, and whether that reaches the
library or only a subset. This module has no Bearer path at all today,
so the cost of finding out is worth knowing before paying it.

