From a5bde1cc46c2fb0bc10751757e96673d8412915f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 09:30:43 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 296/547] Desktop: parse a cookies.txt export into a YouTube
 session

The certain half of the sign-in problem, built while the uncertain half
is researched. Whatever happens with device OAuth, this works today and
cannot rot: the Netscape cookie format is stable, widely exported, and is
what yt-dlp already takes.

Install a cookie-export extension, sign in normally, click, save, pick
the file. That is teachable in one sentence, which "open developer tools,
find the network tab, filter the requests, copy the Cookie header" is not.

The one thing most likely to be got wrong has a test: a leading
#HttpOnly_ marks a real cookie, not a comment, and Google's session
cookies are all HttpOnly. Treating those lines as comments discards
precisely the credential and leaves a file that appears to have parsed.

Only YouTube and Google cookies are taken, and only the session ones. An
export is usually the whole browser, and sending someone's unrelated
session anywhere would be both useless and a real harm.

Three outcomes rather than two: a session, a signed-out export, or an
unreadable file. They need different fixes - sign in, versus export again
- and collapsing them would send the user to correct the wrong thing.

13 tests, including that an empty value does not count as having the
cookie, that the first occurrence of a name wins, and that CRLF does not
break the parse.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
