From febf9e23a0adbc1f50de74d7fa2f726e648c96c4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 10:45:54 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 297/547] Desktop: read a session straight out of Firefox, and
 a lead worth testing

Option B, which for Firefox users removes the export step entirely: one
button, no extension, no file. Firefox keeps cookies in plain SQLite and
has for years, and the driver is already here for the library.

Chrome is deliberately not attempted. Since version 127 its store is
under app-bound encryption whose key is tied to the Chrome process, which
broke every external reader - and chasing that is a commitment to keep
chasing it.

The store is copied before being opened, along with its write-ahead log.
Firefox holds it open in WAL mode while running, so reading in place can
block, can see a half-written transaction, and can leave recovery
journals in someone else's profile. Copying the database without the WAL
would read a state that may be hours old, which for a recently refreshed
session is exactly the wrong one. A test asserts the original is
untouched and no journals are left behind.

Profiles are offered newest-first. An install leaves several behind and
only one is signed in, so ordering by when the store was last written is
the difference between one click and a guessing game.

Only YouTube and Google session cookies are read. This is another
application's credential store and taking more than signing in requires
would not be defensible; a test fails if anything else comes through.

Also added to SIGN-IN.md: OAuth and cookies are not as far apart as they
look. Google has a path - OAuthLogin scope, then uberauth, then
MergeSession - that turns a token into Set-Cookie headers including
SAPISID. If it still works, device OAuth would need no Bearer path in
InnerTube at all; it would mint an ordinary session and every existing
code path would work unchanged.

The likely blocker is named too: OAuthLogin has historically been a
first-party-only scope, which is exactly what was restricted. Recorded as
a lead to test rather than a spec - the endpoints are undocumented and
have changed before.

86 desktop tests.

