From eb8ebf15b0e4970140fa85ac0e4fedccaa1bdd9a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 11:12:06 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 298/547] Desktop: sign in, three ways, all verified before
 they are kept

The routes built over the last two commits now have somewhere to go.
Firefox first, because for anyone using it there is nothing to do at all;
a cookies.txt file second; pasting last, for people who already have a
cookie or use a browser this cannot read.

Every route lands on the same string and every one is checked against the
account endpoint before being stored. A cookie can be well-formed,
contain a SAPISID, and still be signed out, expired, or for a different
property - parsing it proves nothing, so nothing is trusted until
YouTube says whose it is. That is the difference between a sign-in that
works and one that appears to.

Restore happens after visitorData rather than alongside it. Verifying a
stored session is itself a request, and a request made without
visitorData is refused - so restoring first would report a perfectly good
session as expired, which is the most confusing possible answer.

Three states, not two. "Never signed in" needs an invitation; "your
session expired" needs to say so, because otherwise the app has quietly
stopped showing someone's library and their first question is always
whether they are still signed in.

A pasted credential is classified by looking at it rather than by asking:
a tab means the cookies.txt format, anything else is a Cookie header.
Making the user identify their own clipboard is a question that exists
only for the program's benefit.

Stored in the library database under a new credential table, migrated as
schema 2. Not encrypted, and said plainly rather than implied: the key
would have to live beside the thing it protects, and a lock whose key is
taped to the door only makes the storage look safer than it is.

95 desktop tests. The migration is exercised by building a version-1
database by hand, putting data in it and reopening it - a schema step
that only works on a fresh database passes every test written after it
and breaks for every existing user.

