From 55ccc37adb09e3fa38e8dea8ca826b740b901da5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 12:25:09 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 300/547] Sign-in: drive the browser the user already has, over
 CDP

From yt-dlp-getpot-wpc, which is a PO Token provider rather than an
authenticator - but the technique is the piece that was missing. It
drives a real Chrome instead of shipping one.

That inverts the Chrome problem. Reading its cookie store is hard because
app-bound encryption ties the key to the Chrome process. But there is no
need to read the file: the DevTools Protocol exposes
Network.getAllCookies, and a browser answering a question about its own
cookies is not decrypting anything.

Launch the Chrome the user already has with a debugging port and a
user-data-dir of our own, let them sign in - real Chrome, so BotGuard,
2FA and passkeys all work because it is the browser Google expects - then
read the cookies over CDP.

Costs nothing. CDP is JSON over a WebSocket and ktor-client-okhttp
already brings OkHttp, which has one. Against option D's ~150MB of
bundled Chromium, this is a few hundred lines.

The separate user-data-dir is required rather than a workaround: since
Chrome 136 the remote debugging switches are ignored on the default
profile, precisely so malware cannot attach to a real profile and extract
cookies. So the restriction that makes reading the store hard is the same
one that forces this into the shape that works - and that shape is also
the honest one, since the session is created in a profile belonging to
this app rather than lifted out of the user's.

Now the option to build next, ahead of device OAuth: it is certain and
cheap, and it does not hang on a question about Google's policy that
neither of us has been able to settle.

