Subject: [PATCH 301/547] Desktop: sign in inside the user's own browser, over
 CDP

Built from the technique in yt-dlp-getpot-wpc. It is now the first option
offered, because it asks the least: no export, no file, nothing to find.

Reading Chrome's cookie store is a fight - app-bound encryption ties the
key to the Chrome process. But there is no need to read the file. Ask
Chrome. Network.getAllCookies is a CDP method, and a browser answering a
question about its own cookies is not decrypting anything.

So the Chrome that is already installed is launched against a profile
directory of this app's own, the user signs in there - real Chrome, so
BotGuard, reCAPTCHA, 2FA and passkeys all work, because it is precisely
the browser Google expects - and the cookies come back over a WebSocket.

The separate profile is required, not chosen: since Chrome 136 the
remote-debugging switches are ignored on the default profile, exactly so
malware cannot attach to a real one and lift its cookies. The restriction
that makes reading the store hard is the same one that forces this into
the shape that works, and that shape is the more honest one - the session
is created in a profile belonging to this app rather than taken out of
the user's.

The profile is deleted afterwards. Left behind it is a second copy of a
session the app only needed once.

Nothing is bundled. CDP is JSON over a WebSocket and OkHttp was already
here under Ktor; both are now named explicitly rather than relied on
transitively, since a compile dependency on something another module
happens to pull in breaks the day that module changes its mind.

Edge counts, and is included deliberately: it is on every Windows
install, so this works for someone who has never installed Chrome.

12 tests on the part that can honestly be tested without a browser and a
person - what counts as a signed-in session. One caught a real bug:
kotlinx's .jsonArray throws on a shape it did not expect, and the far end
is a browser sending replies to other commands, unsolicited events and
error objects down the same socket. Every step is a safe cast now, so an
unexpected message costs one poll rather than the sign-in.

107 desktop tests.

