From d2bae2ebf16fb5172e7c0964cbd4c490d3c9d5dd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 14:35:32 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 302/547] Desktop: rebuild the player around the Android
 arrangement

Read how the Android player is actually laid out rather than guessing.
Its block order is ARTWORK, INFO, PROGRESS, CONTROLS, ACTIONS, QUEUE, and
it draws a distinction this build had lost: CONTROLS is transport -
shuffle, seek back, previous, play/pause, seek forward, next, repeat -
while ACTIONS is a separate, quieter row for like, equaliser and the
rest. Like was sitting in the transport row here and the seek buttons did
not exist.

The cover now takes whatever height the window gives it instead of a
fixed 360dp, which looked like a postage stamp the moment anyone
maximised. That is what the landscape player does, and it is the whole
reason it puts the art beside the controls rather than above them.

The equaliser moves to a handle at the top edge, pulled down over the
player, which is where the landscape player keeps it. An equaliser is set
once and then forgotten, so a button for it beside play and pause gave it
standing it had not earned. It is still reachable from the actions row -
both routes open the same panel, as on Android - and the open state is
held once, or each entry point would only know about its own taps.

The queue moves to the bottom edge as a bar that is its own button:
collapsed it says what is next, which is the only thing a queue is
usually asked; expanded it lists the lot, bounded so a long queue cannot
push the player off the top.

Album art has two real bugs fixed. A failed fetch was never retried -
the composable only asks again when the song changes - so one dropped
connection left that cover blank permanently, which is exactly the shape
of "the art does not load for some songs". And the full-size cover is a
different URL from the list's small one, so opening the player always
started from an empty tile; it now shows whatever is already decoded for
that cover at any size and sharpens when the large one lands. The cache
also evicts oldest-first rather than clearing wholesale, which was
throwing away covers still on screen.

New icons for seek and equaliser, drawn on the 960 grid because the app
uses Material's for those and there is no drawable to lift. Hand-typed
coordinates are exactly where a mistake hides, so there is now a geometry
test over every icon: parses, inside the viewport, centred in it, sensibly
sized, the seek pair mirror images, the equaliser bars on one baseline.

Its first version measured by pairing up the numbers in the path string,
which is wrong the moment a command is relative - `v-180` is a length,
`l160,-160` an offset - and it duly reported a perfectly good icon as
starting 240 units outside the viewport. Recorded in the test rather than
quietly corrected, because a measurement that cannot read what it
measures gives confident, specific, wrong answers, and the first instinct
is to go and fix the thing that was never broken.

113 desktop tests.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
