From e5024572afd574aa0d4a74eaa44d3295a07ad145 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 15:14:18 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 303/547] Desktop: artist names that lead somewhere

The library kept artists as one joined display string, which the code
already admitted was lossy: "there is no channel to navigate to". So
credits are rows now - an artist table and a song_artist map, the same
shape the Android side uses - and a name can be clicked.

The display string stays alongside them. Every song already stored has
the string and nothing else, and a library that read "Unknown artist"
throughout for anything recorded before this would be a far worse trade
than a name that cannot be clicked.

A credit with no channel keeps a local: id, so the row has a key without
pretending it points anywhere. That distinction is the whole of whether a
name is underlined: plenty of credits arrive as bare names, and a link
that disappoints on the click rather than before it is worse than plain
text.

Credits are replaced rather than merged when new ones arrive - leaving an
old one behind would show somebody no longer on the track - but a song
arriving with none at all is left alone, since a sparse source must not
erase what a richer one supplied. That is the same rule the thumbnail
already follows.

Read back for a whole shelf in one query rather than one per song. Fifty
songs would otherwise be fifty-one round trips for a list nobody has
scrolled yet.

The artist page shows the library's own songs immediately and the remote
ones when they arrive, rather than staying blank until the network
answers - which it would do on every visit, even for an artist already
sitting in the recently-played shelf.

11 tests, including that a version-2 library upgrades without losing
anything, and that songs stored before any of this still show their
artist.

