From 5645911469f675b0472380fa6e82c961cd8e0232 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 15:46:24 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 305/547] desktop: a real equaliser panel, and independent
 tempo and pitch

The panel was 130dp sliders under a header on a screen with room for
several times that. It now leads with the filter bank's actual combined
response - EqResponse evaluates the same coefficients the audio goes
through, so overlapping bands show as the single hump the ear hears
rather than the two separate ones a graph drawn from slider heights
would draw. Sliders are 210dp, gains read to a decimal, and the preset
row went from five to sixteen, because a preset list too short to
contain what someone wants is the same as no preset list.

Tempo and pitch are new and genuinely independent: TimeStretch runs
WSOLA to change duration without touching pitch, then resamples by the
pitch ratio, with the stretch pre-divided so tempo still lands where it
was asked. Both are driven by scroll-wheel dials, which is the gesture
that costs no pointer travel on a desktop.

Two bugs found by measuring the output rather than reading the code:

- The similarity search scanned left to right and kept the first
  maximum. A periodic signal has a matching position every period, so a
  search window a few periods wide has many equal optima and the
  leftmost won every time - pulling the analysis position back by nearly
  the search radius per frame. 1.5x came out at 1.04x. It now searches
  outward from centre, so ties resolve to the smallest nudge.
- The analysis grid advanced from the *chosen* position rather than its
  own fixed grid, so each frame's nudge accumulated. That put 1.5x at
  2.02x, and half speed - where the radius equals the whole hop - at
  forty-four times the audio.

Position reporting is scaled by tempo and rebased when it changes; a
fixed number of line frames per second no longer means a fixed number of
song seconds.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
