From 5aca2a268c175ce63ded06bc4c21e5ade4727153 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 15:52:11 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 306/547] desktop: full compressor settings, with a gain
 reduction meter

Ported from the app's audio/Compressor.kt, which is pure Kotlin and
needed no changes - the two should stay in step, since the same settings
sounding different on desktop and phone would be a bug in one of them.

All five controls rather than an "amount" slider: a one-knob compressor
guesses at threshold, ratio, attack and release on the user's behalf,
and those four are exactly what decides whether it sounds like control
or like pumping. The meter is what makes them usable - threshold and
ratio state a rule, gain reduction is that rule's effect on this track,
and without it the dials get set by reading numbers instead of by
listening.

Placed after the equaliser and before the stretcher. After, so it reacts
to the tone that will actually be heard. Before, because its attack and
release are milliseconds of music: run it after the stretcher and a 10ms
attack would cover 15ms of song at 1.5x tempo, so identical settings
would behave differently at different speeds.

Thirteen tests, all measuring levels rather than inspecting state. One
caught a mistake in the test rather than the code - a stereo expectation
applied the RMS-to-amplitude conversion twice.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
