From 755da44e1eaed2857a979b33c96fe211ef511089 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 16:00:06 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 307/547] desktop: AutoEQ headphone corrections, and the shelf
 filters they need

A correction curve is not a preset. Everything else in the equaliser is
taste; this is a measured error in one specific pair of headphones being
cancelled, and it is the largest single change most people can make to
how their music sounds.

Shelves had to come first. The bank was peaking-only, which was fine
while every band came from this app's own twelve-slider layout - but an
oratory1990 curve almost always opens with a low shelf and closes with a
high one, because a headphone's error at the extremes is a tilt across a
region rather than a bump at a frequency. Approximating a shelf with a
peaking filter gets the centre right and then falls away again exactly
where the correction was meant to keep going. EqBand gains a type, the
biquad gains the RBJ shelf forms, and EqResponse gains them too so the
graph still describes the filter rather than a simpler one.

The parser is deliberately identical to the Android app's, so the same
headphone loads the same numbers on both; only the HTTP client and JSON
parser differ. It carries over the app's hard-won note about the preamp
line: it is one global gain stage, and folding it into each band's gain
leaves a comb of dips rather than a level change.

Two decisions the tests pin down: pass filters are dropped rather than
read as peaking ones, since that would put a bump where the file asked
for a rolloff; and a file with no filters yields null rather than an
empty list, so a failed fetch cannot silently flatten the equaliser.

The sliders now scale to whatever the loaded curve asks for - a fixed
12dB range would have shown a 16dB correction pinned at the top and
reading as milder than what was actually being applied.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
