From b2120e68a464bd9391c93b312e4d61fb564da0b5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 17:04:45 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 312/547] desktop: lyrics, and the equaliser gets the whole
 window

Lyrics needed no porting. `:lrclib` and `:kugou` are already
kotlin("jvm") modules built on ktor - the same stack the desktop uses -
so it depends on them directly. LrcLib first because it matches on title,
artist and duration rather than on a search and returns LRC as-is; KuGou
second because its catalogue is a different corpus rather than a second
opinion on the same one.

The LRC parser is written here rather than reusing the app's, which
reaches a vendored Gramophone file coupled to Android. Fifteen tests,
because lyrics slightly out are worse than lyrics absent - the eye
follows a wrong highlight. Three things a naive parser gets wrong on real
files, each tested: a line can carry several timestamps and dropping the
repeats stops the song following partway through; two-digit fractions are
hundredths, so reading "50" as 50ms puts every line half a second early;
and the offset tag shifts everything, which is the one error every
listener notices.

Results are cached in the library, misses included - songs without
lyrics are common and re-asking both providers on every play of an
instrumental is the most wasteful case there is.

Clicking the cover swaps it for the lyrics, as on Android. Any line can
be clicked to seek to it, which is the thing lyrics can do that a seek
bar cannot. Following pauses while the list is being scrolled by hand,
otherwise reading ahead is impossible - the list yanks itself back every
couple of seconds.

Also, from feedback on the last build:

- The equaliser now covers the window instead of sharing it. A response
  graph, sixteen presets, an AutoEQ search, twelve sliders and seven
  knobs in a strip meant the panel got a scrollbar and the player got a
  sliver. This is what was asked for the first time and I built a drawer.
- The cover sits toward the left of its column rather than centred in
  it, and its corner radius is proportional to its size - a fixed 4dp
  reads as a hard square at half a window wide.
- The progress bar is a plain 3dp line with a small round mark. It is a
  readout that happens to be draggable, looked at constantly and touched
  rarely, so it should be quiet. The value gradient is off it: "how far
  through the song" is not a setting, and colouring it implied the start
  of a track was a low value of something.

