From d26eae48dc98a1d97b1d9c332f00e3635b040952 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 17:10:01 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 313/547] desktop: a settings screen, with sign-in inside it

One scrolling page rather than the phone's tree of sub-screens. That
tree exists because a phone shows about six rows at a time; a desktop
window shows forty, and splitting them would mean navigating to find a
switch that could simply have been visible.

Sign-in moved into it and out of the main navigation. It is setup, done
once, and its old spot beside the library and playlists gave it a
standing it does not have.

Settings are Compose state backed by a key/value table in the same
SQLite file as everything else - so copying that one file backs up the
library and how it was set up together. A key/value table rather than a
column per setting, because a column per setting means a migration for
every new checkbox.

Two things the tests pin down. An unparseable stored value falls back to
its default rather than throwing: a settings layer that refuses to start
because a stored string is not a number turns a cosmetic preference into
a broken install, on exactly the machine where something has already
gone wrong. And writing an unchanged value does not touch the store,
because these are bound to dials that fire on every frame of a drag.

What is now settable: seek buttons, the visualiser, lyrics on cover
click, a lyrics timing offset, colour-by-value, the player background
and whether history is kept.

Backgrounds include the frosted glass that was asked for, plus a plain
blurred cover. Both paint the cover itself rather than colours sampled
from it, which keeps the artwork's own shapes; the scrim over them is
load-bearing rather than decorative, since white text over an unscrimmed
blurred cover is unreadable on anything pale.

The lyrics offset is applied when lyrics reach the player rather than
when they are cached, so changing it costs no re-fetch and the cached
copy stays as the provider sent it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
