From 85aacb7a982a2a145b261325ffba5a50972a6f56 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 10:35:59 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 314/547] desktop: word-by-word lyrics via BetterLyrics

Third provider, and the same story as the first two - :betterlyrics is a
plain JVM module with its own TTML parser, so it is used rather than
ported. Tried first, because word timings are strictly more than line
timings: a word-timed file can always be shown as lines and the reverse
is not true. LRCLIB and KuGou still follow, since BetterLyrics is also
the one most likely to come back with nothing - its API answers 401 for
songs it has not already cached, which is why it is a setting.

Two things it needed beyond plumbing.

LyricWord carries a real end time rather than inheriting the next word's
start. A sung word has a length - held, clipped, or followed by a rest -
and a highlight running straight from each word into the next drifts
ahead of the voice through anything slow.

Enhanced LRC is now parsed for words too, not just stripped of its
markers. The format is far more common than the number of players that
use it, so LRCLIB lines often arrive word-timed for free. Two subtleties
have tests: a bare trailing mark is a line's end rather than a word, and
dropping it throws away a time the file stated; and a repeated line's
word times belong to its first occurrence, so each repeat has to carry
them or every repeat lights up all at once on arrival.

A last word left open is closed against the next line, capped at two
seconds - otherwise the final word before an instrumental break holds
its highlight through the break and reads as the lyrics having frozen.

Rendering fades each word in across its own duration rather than
switching it on. A hard switch reads as a cursor stepping along the
line, and is unforgiving of timings a few tens of milliseconds out,
which most are. Unsung words stay clearly readable - this is a line
someone is reading ahead on.

Format is sniffed rather than taken from the cached source name, since
that name is a claim about where text came from, not about what it is.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
