From 7be2a4faa54f011271b2b5a256cbdcaae3f260f7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 09:57:36 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 315/547] desktop: downloads

The player already fetched a whole MP4 into memory before decoding, so a
download is that same fetch written to a file rather than a second,
parallel way of getting audio. Playing a downloaded song is a file read
instead of a request, and the demuxer, decoder and equaliser cannot tell
the difference. It also never asks YouTube whether the song is still
playable, which is most of the point of having downloaded it.

One resolve path for both, so a download is exactly the bytes that would
have been streamed rather than a second format-selection that can
disagree about which itag to take.

Files are named by video id alone - titles change, get re-tagged, and
contain characters a filesystem will not accept.

The database row is an index and the file is the truth. Three cases the
tests pin down, all of them "the two disagree":

- A record whose file has gone is not reported as downloaded, and the
  stale row is cleared rather than filtered out, so the next request
  downloads it again instead of failing forever on a song the app
  insists it has.
- A save writes to a .part file and moves it into place. An interrupted
  download would otherwise leave a truncated file that looks downloaded
  and fails to decode, which is far harder to diagnose than a missing
  one.
- A leftover .part is never visible as the finished article.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
