From d2f52e78ce06bbd8139916a7f7094196c92ddf84 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 10:08:34 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 316/547] listen together: three fixes found by auditing for
 0.20

Read the whole feature rather than trusting the tests, which all pass
and would have kept passing through each of these.

Cancelling a join was being reported as a failure to reach the host.
CancellationException is an Exception, so the catch around connect
swallowed it - the coroutine then carried on past the point it was told
to stop, and set an error message about a connection problem that had
not happened. It now propagates, as cancellation must.

A follower session ended by cancellation left its socket open. The
link's reader coroutine lives in the manager's scope rather than in the
session's job, so nothing downstream of the cancelled job would ever
shut it down. leave() covers the ordinary path, which is why this never
showed up in use; the finally now closes it either way.

The error card could not be dismissed. "Could not reach Sam's Pixel"
stayed on screen until the next attempt to host or join, so it could sit
above a working session half an hour later and read as a current
problem. Tapping it clears it.

Two things I went looking for and did not find, worth recording so the
next audit does not repeat the search: ListQueue's `position` really is
milliseconds rather than an index, and the self-exclusion check in
discovery compares service names on both paths rather than mixing a
service name with a display name.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
