From 115e7e95a68fc1abc504c73a085444f7c1453442 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 11:12:41 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 317/547] desktop: fix a UI freeze, and colour the whole app
 from the artwork

**The freeze.** download() had no dispatcher and is launched from a
Compose scope, which dispatches on the main thread - so a SQLite check
and a multi-megabyte file write both ran on the UI thread. That does not
look like a slow download; it looks like the app has hung. It now states
Dispatchers.IO, and the two other places that read the download index
during composition were moved off it as well.

**Scrolling.** The equaliser's scroll-to-open sits on the whole player,
so scrolling the lyrics or the queue - both inside it - dragged the
equaliser down over what was being read. It now ignores scrolls another
component already consumed.

**Theming.** The window was a fixed dark scheme while only the player
reacted to the music, which read as the player belonging to a different
application than the library. The whole scheme is now seeded from the
cover of what is playing - the desktop's answer to Material You, since
there is no wallpaper to seed from but there is a large picture the
person chose. Light/dark/system, and a switch to turn the seeding off.

Written by hand rather than by adding material-color-utilities, which
would be a new dependency for one function. The tests found the cost of
that on the first run: green accents failed the readability threshold
where red and blue passed, because HSL lightness is not perceived
lightness - luminance weights green more than three times as heavily. So
accents are searched to meet a contrast target rather than placed at a
fixed lightness, which makes the scheme legible by construction rather
than by luck. Every text-on-colour pair is checked at WCAG AA across
nine seeds including grey, near-black and fully saturated cyan.

