From 153e214ac7fc9b0a61fac2c72881078ca0afc689 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 15:59:25 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 321/547] desktop: reorder and remove queue entries

The arithmetic is extracted into QueueEdit and tested without a player,
for the same reason the artist credit offsets were: this is logic that
fails silently. Get it wrong and the queue still renders and still
plays - it just moves the wrong row, or jumps to another song mid-listen
for no reason the user can connect to what they did.

Everything works in play-order positions, which is what the list shows,
rather than in indices into the song list. Those two are the same only
until shuffle is on, and a function that takes one and means the other
is a bug waiting for the first shuffled queue. Two tests do their whole
job against a shuffled order for exactly that reason.

The case that catches a naive move is dragging a row from below the
current one to above it: everything in between shifts by one, including
what is being listened to, so a position left untouched now points at a
different song. The current entry is found again by its song id rather
than assumed to have stayed put.

Removal has two things that must move together - the song leaves the
list, so order entries pointing past it come down by one; and the order
loses a slot, so a current position after it comes down by one as well.
Doing either without the other leaves the queue pointing at the wrong
song. Playback restarts only when the entry removed was the one playing,
decided by comparing the current song before and after rather than by
comparing positions.

Dragging is by a handle rather than the whole row, since the row already
means "jump to this" and a list where pressing anything might mean drag
is one that cannot be scrolled with confidence. Rows are keyed by song
id so a composable follows its song across a reorder - keyed by position
instead, the drag would be cancelled by its own first step.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
