From 4cb9b555d0c5dfed5a6bae955429c8930f683394 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 16:30:19 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 323/547] desktop: browser sign-in asked for a method that does
 not exist

Network.getAllCookies is a *page* domain method. This connects to the
browser endpoint, which has no page attached, so Chrome answered
"'Network.getAllCookies' wasn't found" - every time, forever. Confirmed
against a real Chrome 152 rather than reasoned about: the browser
endpoint returns -32601 for it and answers Storage.getCookies properly.
Same reply shape, so nothing downstream changed.

Everything around it worked, which is why this was invisible. Chrome
launched, the debugger endpoint came up, the socket connected, and the
sign-in page loaded - only the one command in the middle was wrong.

The second half is worse than the typo. The reply parser was written to
return null on any shape it did not recognise, on the reasoning that one
stray message should cost a poll rather than the whole sign-in. That is
right for a stray message and wrong for an error reply: "there is no
session yet" and "that command does not exist" got the same answer, so
the flow polled an impossible command until the five-minute timeout and
looked to the user exactly like a sign-in being ignored. Protocol errors
are now told apart from an absent session and fail in seconds, saying
what the browser said.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
