From 83f029a0fc7f5895ca1f513f28d7e1ee18b77fe7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 16:35:53 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 324/547] desktop: sleep timer

Two modes, because they answer different questions. A duration is "I am
going to sleep" - stop at roughly that time whatever is playing.
End-of-song is "let this one finish", where the point is the boundary
and a five-minute timer cutting off ninety seconds from the end is the
exact thing being avoided. Setting one cancels the other rather than
both being live, so there is never a question of which wins.

Fourteen tests against a clock the test moves by hand, since every claim
here is about time passing. Two of them matter more than the rest: it
must fire exactly once, because it is polled every second and a timer
left expired would pause playback again the moment anyone pressed play;
and it uses wall clock deliberately, so a machine that sleeps for an
hour wakes with the timer over rather than with forty minutes left.

DesktopPlayer gained a pause() that only pauses. Firing a sleep timer
against togglePause would resume anything already paused, which is the
opposite of the point.

The queue asks a hook at each track boundary rather than knowing about
timers - and only at a natural end, since a stop or a new selection is
the user leaving the song, and ending a session there is not what they
asked for.

The button shows the time left rather than a moon once running: a
control whose whole purpose is "how long until this stops" should answer
that without being opened.

