From 82ca36cd480e12fdb71b60bc8e3004f6673f51e7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 17:04:36 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 326/547] desktop: a probe that says which clients YouTube
 accepts today

Added because I got the last diagnosis wrong by reasoning instead of
measuring. I concluded that signing in broke playback, because the IOS
client is loginSupported and an authenticated device client needs a
proof-of-origin token. That was a plausible story and it was not true.

This probe asks every client the module defines, with no cookie in the
process at all, and all twelve are refused - most with "Sign in to
confirm you're not a bot", the rest with their own variations. So the
session was never the cause: YouTube has tightened this for every client
at once, and a fully anonymous request is refused exactly the same way.

Which means playback on this build is blocked until it can mint a
proof-of-origin token, and no rearrangement of the client list will
help. The phone is unaffected because it already does - see
utils/potoken, which drives a WebView through BotGuard.

Keeping the probe rather than deleting it: "no client worked" is the
least useful error a player can give, YouTube changes what it accepts
without notice, and one run now answers what a reading of the code
cannot.

