From 6c3aab546d7c0e465dd5534f817cfa41331d586b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 17:34:47 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 327/547] desktop: proof-of-origin groundwork - written,
 compiles, does not yet work

Not finished. Committing it because it is real progress and isolated -
nothing in the app calls it, so the build behaves exactly as before.

What is here: CdpSession, which opens a headless Chrome page and
evaluates JavaScript over the DevTools protocol; PoTokenJs, ported
verbatim from the app's JavaScriptUtil (kotlinx.serialization and okio
only, nothing Android); the BotGuard interop HTML copied into desktop
resources; and PoTokenMinter, which runs the five-step flow - Create,
runBotGuard, GenerateIT, build a minter, mint per identifier.

CDP is a better fit than the WebView it replaces. Runtime.evaluate can
await a promise, so the JavaScript-interface callbacks and the
continuation bookkeeping that make up most of PoTokenWebView collapse
into a suspending function that returns a value.

What does not work: the first end-to-end run hangs. Chrome starts and
stays up, and no token comes back, so it is stuck somewhere between
attaching to the page target and the first evaluation returning. Not yet
diagnosed.

One thing found while wiring it that matters more than the hang: the
token is consumed by clients with useWebPoTokens - the *web* clients -
and those return ciphered stream urls that need signature deobfuscation.
The desktop uses device clients precisely because they return direct
urls. So minting may not be the last piece; the app's utils/cipher may
be needed too. Worth knowing before assuming one debugging session
finishes this.

