From 03635209449a0ca9d19e4b9a031ff3a45cb3b95b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 18:20:57 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 328/547] desktop: the minter works, and it turns out nothing
 needed it

Two findings, and the second matters more than the first.

The minter hung because CdpSession attached to the wrong page. Chrome
opens a blank tab before the one it was told to load, and that blank tab
is the page target that appears - so every evaluation ran against
about:blank. Evaluations all *worked*, which is what made it hard to
see: 1+1 was 2, promises resolved, the BotGuard script loaded and
defined its functions. Only the origin was wrong, and origin is the one
thing BotGuard cares about. CdpSession now navigates explicitly and
waits for the document it asked for. A step-by-step probe found this in
one run after reading the code found nothing.

With that fixed the flow works end to end and mints real tokens.

Then the part I nearly got wrong for the third time today. Having made
it work, the obvious conclusion was that the token fixed playback - IOS,
both VISIONOS clients and ANDROID_VR_1_65_10 all returned OK with direct
urls, where an hour earlier every client was refused. But IOS does not
receive a proof-of-origin token at all, so it cannot have been the
reason. Running the same probe with minting skipped gives the same
result: OK, ten formats, ten direct urls.

So the outage was transient - most likely this address being
rate-limited by my own repeated probing - and not the policy change it
looked like. Playback needs no token today.

The minter is therefore committed unwired. It costs a headless Chrome
launch and several seconds, and nothing currently justifies that on the
playback path. It is here, tested, and ready for the next time the bot
check actually bites; a `nopot` switch on the probe makes "is the token
what fixed this" a question that gets measured rather than assumed.

