From 054471450516c1193bbabbffca1c7f862b51c316 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 18:48:02 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 330/547] desktop: import a backup from the phone

Reads the zip the Android app writes: a settings protobuf and a
checkpointed copy of the Room database. Only the database is read - the
settings describe a different application with different options, and
applying a phone's player layout here would be worse than not trying.

Additive and safe to run twice, which was the design decision worth
making deliberately. Songs are keyed by their YouTube id and playlists
matched by name, so re-importing updates rather than duplicates. The
alternative - replacing the local library - turns a merge into something
that quietly destroys whichever device was imported into, and people
re-run imports precisely when they are unsure the first one worked.

Local files are counted and skipped rather than dropped silently. The
phone can play a file on its own storage and this cannot, so those rows
would become entries that fail when clicked; the summary says how many
were left behind. Playlist entries pointing at them are dropped too,
which otherwise leaves a row with nothing behind it.

One thing the schema made easy to get wrong: setLiked rather than
toggleLiked. A toggle called blindly on a second pass turns every liked
song off again - the worst possible outcome for a "just to be safe"
re-run, and a test covers exactly that.

Twelve tests against a database built to the phone's schema, since a
real backup is somebody's library and cannot be a fixture. Database
gained a song(id) read, which the tests needed and nothing had.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
