From 0ba0a0cd95ae203061c76346e4089da3070aa20a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 20:25:08 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 332/547] Downloads: a failed download made its song
 permanently un-downloadable

The reported symptom was tapping Download and nothing happening at all.
This is why, and it is a closed chain rather than a guess:

  - song.dateDownload has exactly one value meaning "not downloaded":
    NULL. Every query is written against it - downloadedOrQueuedSongs()
    is "dateDownload IS NOT NULL".
  - stateToLocalDateTime maps failed, stopped, removing and restarting
    downloads to STATE_INVALID, which is epoch millisecond zero. A real
    LocalDateTime. Not null.
  - scanDownloads writes that straight into the column for every entry
    in Media3's index.
  - so a failed download leaves dateDownload = 0, which "IS NOT NULL"
    returns, which rescanDownloads loads into the downloads map.
  - downloadSong opens with "if (downloads.value[id] != null) return".

One failed or interrupted download therefore made that song
un-downloadable for good, with no error, no notification, and no amount
of tapping changing it. The listener path already guarded this exact
case - "if (state == STATE_INVALID) remove(id)" - but the bulk path did
not, so the guard existed in one of the two places that needed it.

The fix is a nullable downloadDateFor(state, updateTimeMs), which is
what should have been written to a nullable column in the first place.
scanDownloads stores its result directly, so nothing produces the
sentinel any more.

Two places heal what is already stored, which matters more than the
first part: a fix that only stops creating bad rows leaves every
affected song broken. rescanDownloads clears rows sitting at the
sentinel, and the enqueue guard ignores it.

Restarting now counts as in flight rather than invalid; it fell into the
catch-all and briefly showed an active download as absent.

Seven tests, taking the state as an Int so they need no Android - a real
Download needs a DownloadRequest, which needs android.net.Uri. One is a
property over every state Media3 defines, so a new one upstream defaults
to "not downloaded" rather than to something that blocks its song.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
