From 72e3da61926b33ba2af5cbfbd08c8cf5f839d475 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 18:50:33 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 339/547] desktop: fetch the next track while the current one
 plays

Most of the gap between tracks was never the audio line reopening,
which is milliseconds - it was the several seconds spent downloading
the next song, because a song is fetched in full before it plays.
Doing that download during the previous track removes it.

Held as the in-flight Deferred rather than as finished bytes, so a
track that starts while its own prefetch is still running waits for
that fetch instead of starting a second identical one beside it. The
async can never complete exceptionally - a failing one propagates to
its parent immediately, which would take down the scope driving
playback over a song nobody has asked for yet - and a prefetch that
did fail is discarded rather than reported, since it ran minutes ago
against a different network and the right answer to a stale failure
is to ask again.

It waits for the current track to reach Playing before starting.
Both are whole-song downloads over one connection, so firing them
together would make every track start slower in order to make the gap
after it shorter.

QueueState.upcoming decides what to fetch, mirroring next() branch for
branch including the two that are easy to get backwards: repeat-one
replays the current track, and the end of a queue with repeat off has
nothing coming rather than wrapping. Tested, because this is logic
that fails silently - name the wrong song and nothing errors, the
prefetch simply never helps.

Not sample-accurate gapless, and the docs now say so: the line is
still reopened per track and AAC's encoder delay and padding are not
trimmed, so a continuous album keeps a short seam. This is the
difference between seconds of silence and a barely perceptible one.
