From 27aaebc4230a7736362267039e9b709c2cfed14f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 19:12:12 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 341/547] Listen Together: release the listening socket, and
 three smaller fixes
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Four real defects, found by reviewing the feature now that a second
implementation exists to compare it against. None touch the wire
format, so a phone with these fixes and one without still interoperate.

**The host's listening socket was never closed.** LanTransport.listen
launched its accept loop on the manager's singleton scope and handed
back only a Flow, so nothing held the ServerSocket and nothing could
stop the loop. Worse, cancellation would not have helped: accept() is
a blocking call rather than a suspension point, so only closing the
socket underneath it unblocks the thread. The effect was that Start
sharing → Stop → Start left the first port bound, silently fell back
to a random one, and left the old listener still accepting - so a
follower reaching the stale advertisement sat on "Connecting…" for the
full fifteen-second read timeout. listen() now returns a HostListener
whose close() shuts the socket first and then cancels, and the manager
closes it in stop(). Links keep the long-lived scope on purpose: their
writers have to outlive the listener to flush the BYE that
HostSession.stop queues, or every follower would lose the distinction
between "the host stopped" and "the network died".

**A follower's track load outlived the session.** startTrack launched
on the manager scope rather than under run(), so leaving cancelled
neither. playTrack takes seconds and ends by replacing the queue - so
tapping Leave mid-load handed the user the host's song a few seconds
later, in a session they had left. On service teardown the same
coroutine reached a player that had already been released, which is
the crash the ordering in MusicService.onDestroy exists to prevent.
Tracked as loadJob, cancelled with the ping and poll loops, and the
result is discarded if the link closed while it ran.

**ClockSync could lock itself out permanently.** Only accepted samples
enter the window, so the median describes accepted history alone. A
follower that walks out of range and stays there produces samples all
worse than the old median forever - every one rejected, the window
never rolling over, the estimate frozen at conditions that no longer
exist while crystal drift accumulated against it and the UI reported a
healthy session. Five rejections in a row are now read as "normal has
moved" and the window is re-measured. A short burst is still ridden
out; both directions are tested.

**The host browsed for itself.** The nearby list is only rendered when
idle, but the discovery flow was collected in all three modes, so
hosting kept a multicast scan running for a list nobody could see -
the exact battery cost the flow is built cold to avoid.
