From 446aade5b6b42f32ce79675e121ec88535b49c9a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 10:01:45 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 342/547] Listen Together: reconnect after a drop, and join
 without waiting
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two things I flagged as deliberately unfinished last time.

**A dropped link now reconnects.** Previously a WiFi blip ended the
session in complete silence - no message, unlike a host stopping,
which gets one - and rejoining meant walking back through discovery by
hand. The code was clearly designed for this and never got it:
ClockSync.reset is documented "for use on reconnect", and HostSession's
BYE comment says the two cases deserve "different retry behaviour",
which until now meant different wording and nothing else.

join() now loops with backoff (1s doubling to 8s, giving up after 45s)
and keeps the mode on FOLLOWING so the screen holds its place, with a
new reconnecting flag driving a "Reconnecting…" line. The distinctions
that matter are all preserved: a stated BYE reason is a real goodbye
and is never retried; a connect that never succeeded once is "could not
reach", not a drop; and the generation guard still means stopping wins
over anything in flight. Each attempt builds a fresh FollowerSession,
because its clock and drift state describe the link that just died.

**Joining no longer waits for the clock before loading.** onTick bailed
out until four pong samples had landed, so the multi-second song lookup
did not even start for about a second after joining. The lookup never
needed the offset - only the position does - so the load now starts on
the first tick and the position is resolved later.

Which is the second half: playTrack takes the position as a function
rather than a value, and the bridges call it after the lookup, right
before handing the song over. It used to be computed before a load that
then took seconds, so the follower started exactly that far behind and
the next tick "corrected" it with an audible seek - precisely the seek
MediaPlaybackBridge's own comment was trying to avoid. Before the
offset converges it falls back to the tick's raw position, stale by a
fraction of a second, which the nudge band absorbs silently.
