From 66e87f80fbf5ce69f07a771a821e2ebde0b17d9b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 10:38:04 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 344/547] desktop: drag to reorder a playlist

The last list still moving a row at a time by button. Same gesture the
queue already uses, and the same reasoning behind each part of it: a
handle rather than the whole row, since the row is a click target for
playing the song and a list where pressing anything might mean "drag"
cannot be scrolled with confidence; stepped a whole row at a time so
the list settles between moves rather than thrashing while the pointer
crosses a boundary; and keyed by song id so a row follows its song
across a reorder instead of having the drag cancelled by its own first
step.

One difference from the queue is worth knowing, and is why the step
target is coerced against the list the gesture can actually see. The
queue's order lives in a StateFlow and updates synchronously, so the
next drag event always sees the result of the last one. A playlist's
order goes through the database and returns via refreshAll, so a fast
drag can in principle step against a list one frame stale. Coercing to
that list's own bounds is what keeps the tracked index and the list
from disagreeing when it does.
