From 2d4ee45f63be057cdb5430b572c5373fcfbb60c8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 10:50:44 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 345/547] Credit a song with the artist whose page lists it

Asked for: if a song shows up on an artist's Songs page, put that
artist on the song, and collapse a duplicate credit into the one whose
page has it.

The first half is unconditional. YouTube stating, on an artist's own
page, that they perform a track is better evidence than a credit
scraped from a search result - so a missing credit is added, appended
rather than promoted, since being listed says the artist performs the
song and not that they are its principal credit.

The second half is deliberately narrower than asked, and the reason is
already in this codebase. ArtistViewModel.resolveLibraryArtist solves
the same problem at read time and says so plainly: "two artists really
can share a name", so it moves only which row the screen displays and
refuses to second-guess an id that has songs of its own. Writing is a
stronger claim than displaying - a merge repoints every credit and
deletes the row, with no undo and nothing on screen afterwards saying
what was lost. So a duplicate is collapsed only where it cannot be a
different artist: the same channel in YouTube's other spelling, a
locally generated placeholder that never named a channel at all, or a
"- Topic" channel whose stripped name matches. Two real, deliberately
named channels sharing a name are logged and left alone - merging the
composer into the guitarist is not recoverable.

Tested from the refusals inward, since those are what a later
"improvement" would quietly break.
