From a49976d14cbcb56c7f62d8a9852b966763368d31 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 11:03:29 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 346/547] desktop: fix what the prefetch sweep found

A review of the prefetch and icon commits found four real defects in
the prefetch and two in the icons.

**seekTo clamped to zero for the whole of a track's fetch.** durationMs
is 0 until the first frame decodes, and the clamp coerced into 0..0 -
so every seek during the load became a seek to the beginning. That
defeats the thing the pending-seek fix was for: a Listen Together
follower joining mid-song seeks on its first tick, and both the join
position and the correction landed back at zero. Clamped only once
there is a duration to clamp against.

**A claimed prefetch could not be cancelled.** Once play() took it, the
Deferred was reachable only from the decode job's closure - and the two
are siblings, so cancelling the job left the download running. Skipping
a track mid-fetch finished downloading the song nobody would hear,
against the same connection as the one they asked for. Kept in a field
that stop() cancels.

**Claiming it was a read-test-clear across two threads.** play() runs on
the UI thread for user actions and on the decode thread when a track
ends, so it could interleave with dropPrefetch() and await a Deferred
cancelled underneath it - surfacing as a playback failure reading
"DeferredCoroutine was cancelled" after clearing the queue. One atomic
take now, and an await that tells our own cancellation apart from the
prefetch's and falls through to a fresh fetch.

**Repeat-one fetched a second copy of the song already in memory**, and
renewed it every loop. upcoming is right to name the current track -
that is what next() does - but prefetching it holds two full copies
against a 320MB heap, which an hour-long mix would not survive. The
cost is that repeat-one keeps its gap; reusing the decoded bytes would
fix both and is the better answer if it matters.

Two teardown paths also held bytes indefinitely: the sleep timer
halting at a boundary, and end-of-queue after repeat was turned off
mid-track. prefetchJob is volatile now, since startCurrent runs on both
threads.

Icons: the sign-out glyph drew at 12dp, not 18 - size before padding
insets within the box rather than beside it. And `wifi` was defined,
registered and test-exempted but never drawn, because the Android "How
it works" row it was for did not get ported. The row is ported now,
which is worth having on its own: "listen together" sounds like it
streams audio between machines, and it does not.

The test that would have caught that is new, and it immediately found
a second one - queueMusic, unused since the player rebuild, now on the
queue header where the phone has it too.
