From 21ed1e8714d1f4b21582f888c84d2035d5b3014e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: notK50BML <sjiaiclubtwo@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 16:41:37 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 347/547] Harden the artist-page credit feature against its
 edge cases

Six faults, found by walking the paths rather than the happy one.

- Merging moved song credits and then deleted the row, but album_artist_map
  cascades on artist delete and safeDeleteArtist only ever checks
  song_artist_map. An album whose only credited artist was the merged row lost
  its credits entirely, silently and permanently. Album maps are now repointed
  first, as mergePrefixedArtists already did.
- The duplicate's subscription went with it, so an artist the user had added to
  their library quietly stopped being in it. The surviving row inherits it.
- Only the first same-named credit was merged. A song carrying both a "- Topic"
  channel and a placeholder kept one of them, so the name still showed twice -
  with the merge logged as done.
- A same-named set is now all-or-nothing. A placeholder beside a *different*
  real channel of the same name qualified on its own, and merging it would move
  a credit between two real artists on the strength of a name.
- The Topic-channel guard could not fire: the caller stripped the suffix before
  handing the name over, so it only ever caught a title of exactly "- Topic".
  The name now arrives unstripped, and the contract says so.
- Songs were taken from every section of the page, carousels included - "fans
  might also like" and the rest, which are other people's music. Only song
  shelves count now, told apart structurally (carousel songs are always parsed
  with a null album) rather than by localised section titles.

Also: the page's own artist is credited rather than the row on display, since
resolveLibraryArtist may have moved the latter onto a same-named row; a
placeholder page id is refused outright; each song's writes are one synchronous
transaction instead of four fire-and-forget statements; appended credits take
the next free position rather than the credit count, which a merge's gap can
collide with; and unknown song ids are filtered in one query instead of a
relation-loading read per track.

Fifteen new tests across the two policy decisions and the shelf selection, both
extracted so they can be asserted without a database. Verified non-vacuous by
mutation: dropping the Topic check fails three, accepting every section fails
two.
