From e14f6991da4894d109abf7a4857b2fe69b09175c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: notK50BML <sjiaiclubtwo@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 11:29:10 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 350/547] Desktop: bass, balance and treble

Bass and treble are not two new filters. They are a reading of the bands already
there - the average gain at or below 250Hz, and at or above 4kHz - and turning
one writes its value onto every band it covers. That is the phone's rule, and it
is the only arrangement where the dials and the sliders cannot contradict each
other: two extra shelves would give a panel whose graph and whose dials tell
different stories about the same audio.

An AutoEQ correction can have no band at all in one of those ranges, and the
average of nothing is NaN - which would reach the dial as a thumb position and
the filter bank as a gain. That dial is left out instead.

Balance is a new stage, using the phone's own law to the digit: at centre both
channels pass at unity, and panning only pulls the *other* channel down. The
boost-the-near-side alternative makes the track louder as it moves off centre,
which sounds like an improvement and is not one, and has nowhere to go at full
scale but into the clipper. A value past the ends is clamped rather than
inverted - without that, 2.0 gives a left gain of -1, which is inaudible alone
and a hollowed-out centre image the moment anything sums the two.

It runs after the visualiser's measurement point, deliberately: the spectrum is
a mono sum, so applying balance before it would halve every bar at hard left and
read as the music getting quieter rather than as it moving across.

Reading and writing a PCM sample had been written twice inside this module
already, and this would have made three, so both now come from Pcm and
Equalizer's private copies are gone. Byte order is exactly the detail that is
right in two places and subtly wrong in the third, and reading one way while
writing the other is not an error, it is noise. Equalizer's 13 tests still pass
over the shared pair.

Balance and the tone dials persist like the rest of the drawer, on the same
settle delay the compressor's knobs use - a wheel notch has no "up" to hang an
end-of-gesture write on.
