From f0cb1ff5eb86a6e2497b88c098d59a41a91bb30e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: notK50BML <sjiaiclubtwo@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 14:34:08 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 351/547] Fix the Discord cover, and measure the EQ's headroom
 instead of guessing it

Two unrelated faults, both silent.

**The album art.** Every cover was being registered with Discord through a
Cloudflare Worker belonging to another project - inherited with this engine
branch - and that worker no longer exists. Every route on it answers 404 with
Cloudflare error 1042, so getImage returned null, so the asset was null, so the
card rendered without art. Nothing logged it: an unresolved image is a null
field, not an error.

It now calls Discord's own external-assets endpoint directly, with the token the
gateway is already authenticated with. One fewer machine in the path, one fewer
thing to go down, and it stops the cover of everything anyone plays being
announced to a stranger's worker on the way past. Results are cached by URL,
since an album played through asks for the same cover on every track.

lastImageError() exists now too, separately from lastStatus(): the gateway can
be perfectly connected and the art alone failing, and that combination had no
way to report itself at all.

**The equaliser's headroom.** It compensates for its own boosts by attenuating
before the cascade, and the amount was estimated - worst band at 0.8, sum of the
rest at 0.1. Measured against the real response, that estimate is short on every
boosting preset in the app:

  Treble Boost  +3.5dB over     Loudness        +1.9dB over
  Bass Boost    +2.8dB over     Hip-Hop         +1.8dB over
  Small Spkrs   +2.5dB over     Classical       +1.6dB over

So a full-scale track ran up to 3.5dB into the limiter, continuously, on every
peak - heard as grit, and indistinguishable from "that is what a bass boost
sounds like" unless somebody measures it.

The cascade now reports its own peak. Evaluating |H| costs nothing worth
counting - it runs when a slider stops, not per sample - and it is exact for
clustered and spread-out curves alike, which an estimate cannot be: tuning the
weights to stop under-compensating Loudness made them over-attenuate Full Bass &
Treble, because those two differ in exactly the way one weighting cannot
express. The cap rises 12 -> 15dB, since the app's own presets reach +14.5.

With the headroom correct, the limiter threshold goes 0.75 -> 0.9. 0.75 is
-2.5dBFS, which on a modern master is not a peak - it is most of the waveform -
so the limiter had been shaping the signal continuously rather than catching
overshoot.

Seven tests, including one that pins why the estimate was replaced rather than
retuned, so reverting it fails with the reason attached.
