From c112d8903eee1d9fcd60b1633c8641ef5af61e42 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: notK50BML <sjiaiclubtwo@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 15:10:52 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 352/547] Per-song equaliser profiles

A song can now carry its own curve, which replaces the global one while it
plays. The override is complete and authoritative, its own enabled flag
included - that is the point rather than an oversight: what people want from
this is usually "equaliser off, except on this one badly-mastered track", and a
version where the master switch still had to agree could not express it. The
panel says so rather than leaving it to be discovered.

Which store an edit lands in follows what is on screen. With an override active
every adjustment writes to that song; without one it writes the global curve.
Always writing the global one would mean adjustments were silently discarded the
next time the song came round, which is the worst of both.

Applied from the playback service, not the panel, because the panel is usually
not open - a per-song curve that only took effect while somebody was looking at
the equaliser would be a setting that appears not to work. The resolved settings
are what gets distinctUntilChanged, not the inputs, so a track change between two
songs that both use the global curve does not republish coefficients and restart
the processor's interpolation for nothing.

Bounded at 100 songs, oldest-touched evicted first, because this is one
preference string rewritten whole on every change. Writes settle for 400ms
first: without that, one frame of a slider drag would be a six-figure-byte disk
write.

Nine tests, weighted towards eviction - a bound that drops the wrong entry loses
a curve somebody dialled in by ear, silently, with nothing left to say it
existed.
