From ac7f7817681a541496b5cdd750e0e8ee7fc97d7f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: notK50BML <sjiaiclubtwo@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 15:29:46 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 354/547] Wire the exciter to the player, with the cutoff taken
 per track

Inside EqualizerAudioProcessor rather than beside it in the chain: a separate
AudioProcessor would decode every sample out of a ByteBuffer and encode it back
a second time, for a stage that is a handful of multiplies. This one already has
them as floats.

Before the cascade, not after. It is putting back what the codec removed, and
the equaliser should then shape the restored signal like everything else -
running it afterwards would feed the shaper whatever the equaliser had just
boosted, which is a different effect that happens to use the same code.

The cutoff comes from the stream's own format, not from a setting, because that
is what it is a property of: a 128kbps AAC stops around 16kHz and a 256kbps one
around 19kHz. Lossless resolves to no cutoff and switches the stage off, which
is the ask - upscale the lossy, leave the lossless alone.

Off by default. This synthesises content that was never in the recording, and
however carefully it is done that is something to choose rather than discover.

An active exciter now forces the long path, since the bypass bulk-copies the
buffer and there is nothing to work on there; and it resets on a seek, because
carrying its delay line across one rings the old passage into the new.
