From a332b1ded0b7987ec47ef489de285c2cd2c2df61 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: notK50BML <sjiaiclubtwo@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 15:40:54 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 355/547] Lyrics: time the estimated sweep by syllables,
 punctuation and pace

Three changes to how word timings are invented for ordinary line-synced lyrics.

**Syllables, not characters.** The weight was each word's length, which is a
worse proxy than it looks: "through" is seven characters and one syllable,
"away" is four and two. Weighted by length the sweep sits on "through" for
almost twice as long as on "away"; sung, it is the other way up. Two scripts,
because they count differently - in CJK and Hangul a character is a syllable,
and a vowel-group rule returns 1 for an entire line of them.

**Punctuation is time.** A comma is a breath and a full stop is a rest, neither
has any ink, and the old version swept through both at full speed - so the
highlight arrived at the next phrase before the singer did and stayed ahead for
the rest of the line. Each mark is now worth a share, expressed in syllables
rather than milliseconds so it scales with the song: multiplied by this file's
own pace, a comma in a ballad is longer than a comma in a patter verse, which is
exactly what a fixed number of milliseconds cannot do. Capped at 30% of the
line, or a two-word line ending in a full stop would spend most of itself
holding still, which reads as a stall rather than a rest. The last word's
trailing mark is free - there is nothing after it to be held back from.

**A line ends when the singing ends.** A line's end time is really the next
line's start, so a four-bar break is inside it, and the sweep used to crawl
across the words at a quarter speed and finish long after the vocal. The pace of
the whole file - median ms per syllable, median because the tail is entirely
made of lines that end at a break - says how long the words themselves take, and
the sweep finishes there and holds. Capped by the line's real duration, so this
can only ever finish early, never run past the point where the next line has
started.

Fifteen tests, written around the properties rather than exact milliseconds.
Verified load-bearing by zeroing the pause weights, which fails two of them.
