From 8fe785384b3635672a9b448e2dc505572c67a1c3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: notK50BML <sjiaiclubtwo@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 18:23:30 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 356/547] Make the exciter actually audible, fix a crash, and
 drop 4MB of video libraries

**It was doing nothing.** A probe over brickwalled noise - what a lossy stream
really looks like, rather than the loud tones the tests used - measured all
three strength settings producing identical output to four decimal places. A
soft saturator's harmonic output grows as the square of its input, and the band
being fed here is the top octave of already-quiet material, sitting near -55dBFS
where the curve is indistinguishable from a straight line. It generated 2.6dB,
which is the measurement floor rather than an effect.

A drive stage fixes it: the band is pushed into the region where the curve
actually bends, and the saturator's own ceiling is what makes driving it that
hard safe. Measured on the same signal, the restored band now lands 16.5dB
(Subtle), 12.2dB (Balanced) and 8.2dB (Strong) below the 8-15kHz band, with the
midrange unchanged to 0.00dB at every setting.

Three tests to stop it coming back - one that the settings differ at all, one
that realistic band-limited content is excited by a usable amount, and one that
the midrange is untouched at every strength. The tone tests all passed
throughout; they could not have caught this.

**A crash.** The filter arrays were fixed at two channels while the bounds check
compared against the stream's real count, so a 5.1 file would have indexed a
two-element array from the audio thread. They are sized by configure now and the
check is against the array.

**A setting being consumed rather than applied.** configureExciter cleared
`enabled` whenever a stream had no gap to fill, so one lossless track switched
the feature off and every lossy track after it played flat with the switch still
reading on.

**4MB per ABI of video libraries.** Measured from the ELF headers, not assumed:
libffMetadataEx's DT_NEEDED list does not include libavfilter, and the only
library that does name it is libavdevice, which nothing names at all. Neither is
dlopened - there is no "libavfilter" string in the binary, and zero avfilter_ or
sws_ symbol references. Excluded from packaging, which needs no rebuild of the
AAR because nothing has to be relinked.

libswscale stays for now. It is equally dead - zero sws_ references - but it IS
in the DT_NEEDED list, so the loader refuses to open the library without it.
That 762KB needs ffMetadataEx relinked without -lswscale.
