From cc626488df8097393ce22ff4bf5c666265088a82 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: notK50BML <sjiaiclubtwo@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 18:35:21 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 357/547] Lyrics: honour the provider order, and re-check on an
 interval

**The preference only decided who was asked.** In AUTO mode providers are raced,
and the aggregator kept the first result of each quality class to arrive -
`if (heldSynced == null)`. Arrival order is network latency, so the quickest
provider won every tie and the order at the top of the list was never consulted
about who to believe. It looked intermittent because it was: whoever answered
first that day.

Results are now ranked by quality first and by the user's order second. Quality
still wins outright - a lower-ranked provider with word timings beats a
preferred one with line timings, because a line renderer ignores word marks and
the richer result is strictly more. Order only breaks ties, which is what a
preference list is for.

Stopping early still costs nothing. The race ends as soon as a word-synced
result is held *and every provider ranked above it has reported* - which for the
top provider is immediately, the common case. A lower-ranked one waits, bounded
by the deadline that was already there.

Knock-on: the cache signature now carries the provider order in AUTO too. It
used to sort them, on the reasoning that order could not change the answer
there. That was true of the code and is no longer, so a cache produced under one
order really is stale under another.

**And a re-check interval.** Nothing ever looked again at a result that was
found, so a song that only had a plain text sheet when first played kept it
forever, even after a provider gained a word-timed version. Weekly, fortnightly
or monthly, off by default.

Per song as it is played, not a sweep over the library: a daily pass over a few
thousand songs is thousands of requests to three third-party services for an
answer that has almost always not changed, and it would run whether or not
anyone was going to read them.

The risk a re-check introduces is coming back with *less* than is stored -
providers go down, races go differently - and replacing word-timed lyrics with
plain text would make the feature worse than not having it. A result that is
worse than what is held is refused and only the check time is recorded. An equal
one is taken, since that is how moving a provider up the list eventually takes
effect.

Twelve tests on the ordering, eleven on the interval and the downgrade rule.
Verified load-bearing by reverting the tie-break to arrival order, which fails
three.
