From e720aa52cce31ef805af1eaeec899b0e26ca6177 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: notK50BML <sjiaiclubtwo@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 18:45:12 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 358/547] EQ: a response curve, readable faders, and AutoEQ
 that says why it failed

**A response curve.** The panel had twelve faders and no picture of what they
add up to, which is both why it looked like a row of sliders rather than an
equaliser and why it could not answer its own question: the bands overlap, so
two adjacent boosts make one larger hump rather than two small ones, and no
arrangement of slider positions shows that.

The curve is the real filter bank, not a drawing of one. Every point is
BiquadCoefficients.cascadeDb over the coefficients forBand produces - the same
function, from the same inputs, that the audio thread runs on every sample. The
alternative, re-deriving the cookbook formulae for display, gives a graph that is
right until one of the two copies is edited and then silently graphs a filter the
app is not applying. (The desktop does it that way and says so in its own doc;
this is the better half of the two.) Pass filters are included for the same
reason - a low-pass has no gain at all, so a curve that skipped zero-gain bands
would draw a flat line over a filter removing half the spectrum.

**Faders that fill from the middle.** A band's centre is 0dB, not "none", and
filling from the end made a flat band look half full - half of what? From the
centre, a flat band is an empty channel, a boost grows up and a cut grows down,
and a row of twelve shows the shape of the curve without anyone reading a
number. Plus faint detents to judge position against and a grip line across the
handle. Deliberately not more than that.

**AutoEQ was rate limiting itself out of existence.** Every search spent three
of the sixty requests an hour an unauthenticated caller gets - one per category
- so about twenty searches exhausted the budget. The listings are folder names
in a public repository that change when somebody adds a headphone, so they are
now cached for an hour: a session costs three requests rather than three per
search.

And being rate limited looked exactly like finding nothing. `if
(!response.isSuccessful) return emptyList()` turned a 403 into an empty result,
so the screen said no headphones matched when the truth was it had not been
allowed to look. Rate limiting, an unreachable network and a genuine absence are
three different messages now, because "nothing found" sends someone off to check
their spelling and the other two do not.

Ten tests on the curve maths, including the pass-filter case and the cliff a
22kHz file would otherwise draw at Nyquist.
