From 7cda435a00877ed81d3b24e519bafca9979070a5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: notK50BML <sjiaiclubtwo@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 19:26:50 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 359/547] Colour the EQ curve by height, and finish the caption
 cleanup
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

**The curve takes its colour from its own height.** Yellow where it is cut,
blue where it is boosted, green at unity, so the shape carries its own reading
of itself. Keyed to the canvas rather than to the curve, because the plot's
scale already follows the curve - letting the colours follow it too would make a
+3dB bump look identical to a +12dB one.

**The faders now run the same way.** They were blue at the bottom of a range and
yellow at the top, which is backwards twice over: turning a control up makes the
sound brighter and thinner, which is what blue reads as, and with the curve read
by height a boost would have been yellow on the graph and blue on the fader that
caused it. The desktop has always run this direction; the phone was the odd one
out. One line to put back - the two colours in valueGradientColor.

**The caption cleanup only caught half of what it was aimed at.**

A single leading `>` was left alone. YouTube uses `>>` for a change of speaker
and a lone `>` where the same one carries on, and the rule required two - so
every continuation line kept its bracket. A `>` in the middle of a line is still
left alone, because out of the position the convention puts it in it is far
likelier to be something the lyricist wrote.

Notes wrapped around a line that has words in it are gone - `♪ Never gonna give
♪` reads as decoration applied to every line rather than as a mark that means
anything. A line that is only notes is untouched: that one is the mark.

`[Instrumental]`, `[Guitar solo]` and the recogniser's own `[inaudible]` family
are handled now too. The first two earn the note placeholder like `[Music]`
does; the last are removed outright, because they describe a failure of the
transcript rather than an event in the recording and there is nothing for a
placeholder to stand in for.

**And it now runs on the way to the screen, not only on the way to the
database.** That is why it looked like it was not working: the fetch path
cleaned what it stored, which covers everything fetched from then on and nothing
else - a row written before that existed kept its `[Music]` forever, and a local
.lrc file never went through the fetch path at all. It is idempotent, so running
it twice costs nothing. TTML is exempted and cleaned after conversion instead,
since it is markup and the leading-`>` rule would eat any wrapped tag.

Eighteen tests, including two pinning that enhanced LRC survives - that format
is full of angle brackets, and treating `<00:01.00>` as a speaker marker would
silently downgrade a word-timed sheet to a line-timed one.
