From 1cbd9652781a2fe3c0b1d99d9fa39ba02685bd3c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: notK50BML <sjiaiclubtwo@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 20:55:41 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 360/547] EQ panel: three panels in reading order, and one set
 of fonts

Reordered to the shape asked for. Equaliser first - its name and switch, then
every curve that can be loaded, then the faders, then the band editor under the
band it belongs to, then the response curve as their result, then what it all
applies to. Compressor second. Additional settings last: the controls that shape
a curve without being one, and everything to do with keeping it.

Switching the equaliser off now collapses its panel rather than dimming it,
which is what the compressor already did. Twelve dead faders on screen read as
broken rather than disabled.

**A "Current song" chip.** In the row only while the per-song switch is on,
because that is the only time it refers to anything - it is not a stored profile
with a name, it is a label for whatever is playing, which is why it has no
delete button. Picking any other curve turns the switch off, since a panel
claiming a song has a private curve while plainly showing a preset is lying
about one of the two.

**Fonts.** "Equalizer", "Compressor" and "Additional settings" are peers and now
say so - the compressor's heading was labelMedium against the equaliser's
titleLarge, which made one of three sibling panels read as a sub-heading of
whatever was above it. Everything else settles on labelLarge for a label that
names something and labelMedium for a readout of a value; titleSmall sat between
the two and belonged to neither.

**Green where white was meant.** Four controls took a hardcoded green rather than
the panel's contrast colour, so turning colour-by-value off left them green
while everything around them went white. The constant is gone rather than
narrowed - with it there, the next control would have reached for it too.

**Save through Import in one scrolling row**, which is what they are: six
variations on "keep this curve", previously stacked into a block of buttons
taller than the controls they were about.

The two display toggles are out of the panel - they already existed in Settings
> Equalizer and the panel was a second copy. A new option there puts them back
in Additional settings for anyone who wants them without leaving the player, and
another saves profile edits automatically instead of waiting for Save, off by
default because silently rewriting a preset being auditioned is a surprise.
