From c0e04293cfc3edd20eadb687326fdb52ceabc3cf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: notK50BML <sjiaiclubtwo@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 19:08:55 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 362/547] The equaliser is a real drawer, and bass/treble are
 real shelves

**A drawer, using the queue's own physics.** It used to slide in on a boolean,
so the only way to open or close it was to hit the handle exactly - a swipe did
nothing, and a quick one did nothing twice over because there was no gesture to
interrupt. TopSheet is BottomSheet upside down and is built on the same
BottomSheetState rather than a state of its own: the spring it settles with, the
velocity above which a flick counts as a flick, the overshoot handling, all of
it is shared. That is how "opens exactly like the queue" is achieved rather than
asserted - a second implementation would start identical and drift.

Three things are mirrored and nothing else is: the offset is negative, the drag
delta is negated because open is downward here, and the fling velocity is not
sign-flipped because a downward drag is already positive. Swiping the panel up
closes it, which is the other half of what was asked for.

Binding is two-way on purpose. The button beside the like button and the
pull-down handle both set `visible`, and a drag sets the sheet - so each tells
the other, or the button stops working the first time somebody swipes the panel
shut. The content is not composed while the sheet is closed: twelve faders, a
response curve and a compressor would otherwise be built behind a shut drawer on
every track change.

**And the tone controls are shelves.** They were a shortcut that wrote one flat
gain onto every band at or below 250Hz and at or above 4kHz, which is not what a
tone control is and showed in both directions: the faders jumped whenever a knob
moved, and the result was a lump centred on those bands rather than a curve
rising toward the edge of the spectrum. They are a low shelf and a high shelf
now, at the same corners, kept out of the fader strip.

One function, allFilters(), is what the audio, the response curve and the
headroom measurement are all built from - three things have to agree about what
the equaliser is, and the moment any of them builds its own list the picture
stops describing the sound. Schema 3; a profile written before shelves existed
reads as having none, which is right, because such a file expressed its tone
through the band gains and those are still in it.

Eight tests on the shape, including that a shelf levels off past its corner
rather than coming back down - which is exactly what distinguishes it from the
wide band it replaced.
