From 8efb65a5e83d3e4d043ae36a761615e57a5b79c0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: notK50BML <sjiaiclubtwo@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 19:30:15 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 363/547] Stop the response curve rescaling itself out from
 under the drag

The plot scaled to its own contents and rounded the scale up to the nearest
decibel, so dragging a band changed the scale on almost every frame. A plot
whose scale tracks its contents cannot show the contents growing: the curve
stayed the same height and shimmered as the axis stepped underneath it, which
reads as the control having stopped working rather than as a rescale.

Four fixed steps now - 6, 12, 18, 24dB either side - so the scale changes a
handful of times across the whole range instead of continuously. Between steps
twice the boost is twice the height, which is the entire point of a response
plot, and the rescales that do happen are rare enough to animate rather than
jump.

Desktop: the generated theme takes its tertiary accent from a second colour in
the cover rather than a fixed -30 degree shift off the first. Every accent
sharing one hue is exactly why a generated window reads as a single wash of
colour - Material You pulls several colours out of the image and builds its
accents from different ones, and that is the difference between tinted and
themed. The clusters are searched in order of how much of the cover they occupy
and the first far enough round the wheel wins; a cover with only one colour in
it falls back to precisely the old behaviour, which matters because those are
common and must not get worse.
