From 84b558dc75acb01f25bb7e2627c658929fa08124 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: notK50BML <sjiaiclubtwo@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 20:07:30 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 365/547] Spend normalisation as equaliser headroom, dock the
 queue, unstarve the tests

**Boosts are free now, up to the headroom the track already had.** Loudness
normalisation turns most streams down six to ten decibels, and that attenuation
is room between the signal and full scale - but it was applied after the
equaliser, at the player's volume, where the equaliser could not reach it. So a
boost bought headroom the track had already been given, and the two were paid
for twice.

The attenuation is handed to the processor instead, and the pre-gain takes the
larger of the two rather than their sum. An 8dB boost on a track coming down
8dB now needs no further attenuation at all: the band ends where it started and
the rest ends where an un-equalised normalised track would have been anyway. It
used to end 8dB down. Only a boost larger than the headroom costs anything, and
the band still never exceeds full scale.

Applied in exactly one place either way - the processor when the equaliser is
on, the volume stage when it is off, since with the equaliser off there is
nothing to spend it on and doing it per-sample would mean touching every sample
of every track to multiply by a constant.

**The desktop queue docks beside the player** past 1000dp instead of covering
it. A queue that hides the player is right on a phone and wrong on a window a
metre across - the thing being played disappears in order to look at what is
playing next. It slides in from the side it occupies rather than up from an
edge it is not anchored to, and jumping to a track no longer closes it, because
having both on screen is the point.

**And SessionTest is no longer starved.** It scoped onto Dispatchers.IO - one
64-thread pool for the whole JVM - while LanTransportTest in the same suite
opens real sockets on it, and ServerSocket.accept() blocks a thread rather than
suspending. Run together, those blocked threads were enough that this test's
coroutines were never scheduled: a twenty-second timeout waiting for a handshake
it had already asked for, passing every time it ran alone. Each test gets its
own pool now, shut down with its scope so the idle threads do not become the
same problem. Three consecutive full-suite runs green, two of them forced.
