From 9f3063e17655069584c9baf75390fe710c2ee72b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 13:02:09 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 408/547] Desktop: ferrofluid player background

A pool of ferrofluid along the bottom of the player that the music magnetises. Built on what the
real thing does over a magnet rather than on a pulse: flat until the bass passes a critical field,
then a hexagonal lattice of rounded cusp spikes erupts (the Rosensweig instability); the field is an
under-damped spring driven by the bass, so the crown overshoots, rings and slumps back; tips lean as
the field drifts and tremble with the treble. Shaded as a black mirror reflecting a studio lit in the
cover's colours, on a dark glossy plate.

Raymarched as a height field in SkSL: the march is bounded to the pool's cylinder in closed form,
the step adapts to field strength, it renders at 60% resolution, redraws at most ~40 times a
second, and stops redrawing entirely once the pool is at rest with nothing playing.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
