From 9c7d1d74875cc6a104fb7e5b7b0acea82dabdeac Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 13:12:42 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 434/547] Ferrofluid: let the loud hits hit harder, and answer
 the voice

Two changes, both about what the fluid is reacting to.

The kick's share of the spike height goes from 16 to 26. Loudness reaches the
spikes two ways: the pool, which is square-rooted on purpose so a quiet passage
still moves it and a loud one does not sit pinned at the ceiling, and the
onset impulse. Raising the pool would spend that whole range at once; raising
the impulse only makes hits hit harder, which is what "the loudest sound throws
the biggest spike" actually asks for, and it leaves the floor where it was.

And a second onset detector on the mid band, where a voice sits - the same
rising-faster-than-its-own-average test the bass already uses. It drives a
ripple travelling outward across the pool, deliberately not the spikes: the bass
is the magnet, and a voice pulling on the crown would set it moving against the
pulse everybody can hear.

Reading syllables off the lyrics was the other way to do this and is worse. It
needs synced lyrics to do anything at all, so instrumentals get nothing; most of
what does have them has estimated timings, which drift against the audio; and it
tracks the vocal line, which is often not the loudest thing in the mix. Onset
flux on the mids needs none of that and is measuring the same event.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
