From ba2123c8b7ec4aa6a699b9d66665be4808f60c3d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 13:12:42 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 435/547] Updater: a desktop release must not stop the phone
 finding an apk

This repository publishes the Windows build into the same releases as the
Android one, and the check read /releases/latest. A Windows release carries no
apk, so whenever one was newest, the scan looked in it, found nothing for this
device, and returned null - which is the same answer as "you are up to date".
Android updates would have gone quiet for as long as that release stayed on top,
with nothing to show for it.

It reads the list now and walks back until it finds a release with an apk for
this abi and flavour, so a release with nothing for this device is skipped
rather than treated as the end of the search. Drafts and pre-releases are
skipped explicitly, which is what /releases/latest was doing for us.

Nothing here could ever have downloaded the .exe - ASSET_NAME is anchored at
both ends with .apk inside the pattern, so OuterTune-setup.exe and
OuterTune-windows.zip fail the match outright rather than being filtered out
afterwards by a check that a later edit could reorder. Said so in the doc, since
that is now load-bearing for two platforms rather than one.

