From f7971899d2d2b0759eff8a09512b5b0465ed93e6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: v <v@l>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 13:51:36 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 438/547] Desktop: the phone's mini player, and a Privacy
 screen worth the name

Mini player: the phone's numbers, from its Dimensions.kt - 64dp tall, 48dp
artwork at 6dp rounding with 6dp around it, title 16sp bold and artist 12sp
secondary. The progress line moves to the bottom edge, where it underlines the
bar instead of ruling it off from the content above, which is what stacking it
in a Column amounted to.

The part worth having is the artwork carrying the state. Loading and failure
used to go in the artist line, so a song that would not load read as being by
somebody called "no internet connection". They now sit on the thing that is not
loading, as a spinner or an error mark over a dimmed cover. The reason still
appears in words, because the mark says only that something went wrong.

The background stays, which the phone does not have: its mini player sits on the
navigation bar and is already separated from the list. Here it floats over
scrolling content and would read as part of it.

Privacy: each kind of history now owns its switch and its clear button, instead
of the switches sitting in one section and the clearing in another - pairing
them is what makes "stop recording this, and get rid of what was recorded" one
thought. Both clears are two-click, as the listening one already was.

Sharing listening history with YouTube Music is new - the desktop has never
reported a play to the account at all. It is off by default, which is the one
place this does not follow the phone: there the setting is a pause switch and
reporting is on because the phone has always done it. Turning it on in an update
would mean quietly starting to write to somebody's YouTube history. The row is
absent rather than disabled when signed out, since a switch that cannot do
anything reads as broken rather than as not applicable - and the desktop can be
exact about that, because its sign-in is a verified account call rather than the
phone's "is there a SAPISID in the cookie" guess.

Proxy, which had no desktop equivalent at all. Applied on change rather than
only at start-up - the phone needs a restart because it reads the setting once
in App.onCreate. Requests already in flight are not moved across.

