From 6928041aae5fe74b9062649567bb09b0fc5510f2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: v <v@l>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 13:57:32 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 439/547] Desktop: audio quality, seek size, fade length, local
 scan, automatic backups

Settings the phone has and this did not, each replacing a constant that was
never anybody's choice.

Audio quality picks between itags 139, 140 and 141 - the same AAC-LC in MP4 the
decode chain is built around, at roughly 48, 128 and 256 kbps - so it changes
the bitrate and nothing downstream. Automatic stays on the itag the probes
settled on rather than the biggest number published, because "known to work
here" is what automatic should mean; High falls back to it when a track offers
nothing larger. Anything outside AAC-in-MP4 is still only a last resort.

Seek size and sleep-fade length were hardcoded at five and thirty seconds.

Local music gets an enable switch and a rescan-at-start switch. Rescanning
unconditionally at every launch is what a large local library paid for with no
way to decline; loading what is already known is not a scan and still happens.

Automatic backups are the one here that is not parity for its own sake.
Everything this app knows - plays, likes, playlists, settings - is in a single
SQLite file, and the only copy of it was the one somebody remembered to export.
That is the wrong shape for a single file: the moment it matters is the moment
it is already gone. Backups are written beside the library, pruned by count
oldest-first, and whether one is due is decided from the files on disk rather
than a stored timestamp - a recorded "last run" and an actual backup can
disagree, and the files are the thing being relied on.

Checked hourly rather than only at start-up: the interval is in hours, and a
desktop app is left running for days, so a start-up-only check would never fire
for the people it matters most to.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
