From 9afa14cdcb9ef69fd3d2405831e4595f4aba1e7e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: v <v@l>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 11:52:42 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 440/547] Desktop equaliser: faders instead of lines with
 handles, and the spectrum behind

The bands were a rotated Material slider - a six-pixel line with a round thumb
riding on it. Correct control, wrong object: what a fader actually is is a
channel cut into a panel with a cap in it, and a line with a dot on it reads as
a setting rather than as a mixing desk. Ported from the phone's PowerampSlider,
which already had this right: a rounded channel, faint detents to judge a
position against, fill growing from the centre so a flat band is an empty
channel rather than a half-full one, and a cap with a moulded grip line across
it so it does not read as a capsule.

And the analyser, which is the thing that makes a curve worth having. Twelve
numbers and a response shape tell you what the filter bank is doing; they do not
tell you whether it matters. A cut at 3kHz means nothing on its own and means a
great deal when 3kHz is visibly where the energy is. The tap already existed for
the player's visualiser and the ferrofluid - this only draws it.

Placed on the curve's own log axis rather than linearly, so a peak in the
analyser sits over the part of the curve that governs it. Linear placement would
crowd everything audible into the right-hand third and make the two disagree
about where any given hertz is, which is worse than drawing nothing. Faint and
drawn first, so it stays a backdrop rather than a second line competing with the
response.

Sampled in an effect rather than inside the Canvas. The tap is a queue that
drains as it is read, so sampling during a draw pass is a draw pass with a side
effect - Compose may run it twice and the second read gets a different answer,
which shows up as the analyser dropping frames at random.

A setting, on by default, because it is a per-frame redraw of the panel and
worth declining on a slow machine.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
