From c8cdb61ed5f06368dd9921bf5bcad1703665aa08 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 20:22:46 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 481/547] Windows install: 105MB to 95MB, by shipping only what
 runs

Measured rather than guessed: jdeps over the shrunk jar for the Java modules the code reaches, and a
look at what in the runtime folder is ever loaded.
- Modules nothing uses are gone: java.net.http (ktor talks through OkHttp), java.naming, PKCS#11,
  jdk.net, and jdk.localedata - the interface is English, so dates and numbers are now written the
  English way too, from what java.base carries.
- The Universal C Runtime DLLs (api-ms-win-*, ucrtbase), which Windows 10 never loads from an app
  folder, and the runtime's developer tools, link libraries and PostScript tables.
- SQLite's and JNA's natives for 32-bit and ARM Windows, which a 64-bit x86 runtime cannot load.
  The plain fat jar keeps them for anyone running it on their own Java.
- Modules compressed at zip-9.

Checked on the trimmed runtime against a copy of a real library: the window opens and loads the
home feed, lyrics are fetched, a downloaded song decodes and plays. The installer is 72MB; an
update removes the dropped files through its manifest.

