From 2e95146d0284469a098deaa1963ed623167ff7e9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 20:36:13 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 482/547] Desktop playback: streams resolve through NewPipe,
 the way Flow does

Every song had stopped playing. YouTube now answers the device clients the player used with "This
video is unavailable" - or, for iOS, with a URL that serves the first megabyte and then 403s, which
is how googlevideo treats a client it wants a proof-of-origin token from. Measured today, with a
1MB range request and then the rest: iOS 200 then 403; every other client refused outright.

NewPipeExtractor resolves the way the web player does, deciphering the signature and the throttling
parameter with the player's own JavaScript, and its URLs serve the whole song (2-7MB in 2-4s). It is
how Flow plays music, and it was already bundled - the Android engine uses it as a last resort - so
this costs nothing in size. It is tried first now, with the old clients kept behind it.

NewPipe gives no loudness figure, so the iOS player response is asked for it alongside; it answers
well inside NewPipe's time. Songs start 2-3s after pressing play, checked on the trimmed runtime.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../dd3boh/outertune/desktop/DesktopPlayer.kt | 66 +++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 59 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

