From cc965951f5e56b2ee7c69b049075dc6e8e10c3a5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 11:08:54 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 496/547] Desktop: updates from GitHub releases, installer only

The phone's updater, for Windows. At start (at most every six hours, on by default) it reads the
project's releases and offers a newer version in a bar above the player and in Settings > About,
where it can also be checked for by hand. Download automatically is a setting, off by default.

What it will fetch and run is deliberately narrow:
- only an asset named OuterTune-setup-<x.y.z>.exe, from a published release - never an APK, zip,
  MSI or anything else, and no drafts or prereleases;
- only from GitHub, redirects included;
- only if it is the size GitHub lists, matches the SHA-256 GitHub publishes for it when there is
  one, and is a Windows program (starts with MZ).
It downloads into its own folder in the system temp directory, which is emptied at every start, so
the installer - which cannot delete itself while it runs - is gone the next time OuterTune opens.
Installing runs it silently as an update, closing the app (likes still waiting are sent first);
the installer opens OuterTune again when it is done.

The installer is now named with its version (OuterTune-setup-0.3.0.exe), which is how a release
asset is recognised.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
