From 7c1a12f691f66a8807fe823ea61830bf80d8a88d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 18:11:10 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 512/547] Equaliser: turn the volume up when it only cuts, on
 both platforms

A boost has always been paid for by turning everything down by the curve's highest point, so the
song cannot clip. A curve that only cuts got nothing back: turn every band down and the song just
got quieter, and the way to fix it was to add gain by hand. Now such a curve is raised by its own
shortfall (up to 12dB), so its loudest point sits back at the song's level - "Turn up for cuts",
on by default, beside the headroom setting (Windows: Settings > Player and audio).

Measured from the curve's highest point between 20Hz and 16kHz, so it can never lift anything above
the level the song arrived at, and a curve that cuts some bands and leaves others at zero is left
alone - quieter where it was cut, as asked. 16kHz because these filters drift back towards 0dB near
Nyquist: a curve cut 10dB everywhere audible measured as cut by 1dB at the very top, and the phone's
peak measurement never went below zero at all, so neither would have raised anything.

