From 04c762d8cb214afab3a8196331e90c5b7ec55767 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 18:24:37 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 516/547] Android downloads: whole covers, removed downloads
 kept in the cache, no more freezes

Covers. A downloaded song's cover was written straight into place, so it could be read half-written
- the player drew its top half and kept showing that - and a dropped connection or two fetches of
the same cover at once left a cut-off file for good. Covers are now fetched one at a time per song
into a file of their own, checked (expected size, a JPEG's end marker) and only then moved into
place, falling back to the song's own size when YouTube has no full-resolution image. Covers
already cut off are found and fetched again at start. Downloads always save the full-resolution
cover now, so its setting is gone; a song that already has a whole one is not fetched again, and
song rows use the saved cover instead of fetching a low-resolution copy.

Removing a download keeps the song in the song cache when the cache has room - still playable
offline until the cache needs the space, and downloading it again needs no fetch (the reverse of
the existing cache-to-download promotion). Settings > Library: Keep removed downloads in the song
cache, on by default; off deletes it completely. Every screen removes downloads through this one
path, which also deletes the saved cover.

Freezes with several downloads at once:
- every download state change wrote the song table, even writing "not downloaded" over a song
  that already was not, and each write re-ran every live song query in the app; queued and
  in-progress changes no longer write anything;
- every song row watched the whole downloads map and redrew on every change to any download; rows
  now watch only their own song;
- every downloaded byte was also written into the song cache, then deleted - doubling flash writes
  and pushing the songs being streamed out of the cache;
- the stream-URL cache was a plain HashMap shared by five download threads.
Also: a request whose start was cached but not the rest went out unresolved and failed until a retry.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
