From fe3aaa2567638b797e2ce1767e2742180bdc2fdd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <t@t>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 18:51:58 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 521/547] Artist credits: the enricher never drops or swaps a
 credit on a guess

The enricher could not tell "no such artist" from "could not ask". Offline - the relink sweep at
start, playback with no connection, a download rescan, local files - every search came back
empty, so a combined "X & Y" credit was judged fake and dropped with no halves to replace it. It
also kept whichever half of a split matched, so "Mumford & Sons" could become some channel called
"Sons". Searches now answer Found, NotFound or Unknown; nothing is removed on Unknown, nothing is
split offline or on a local file's own artist, and "X & Y" is only replaced when every half is a
real channel. A credit to a lesser-known channel was repointed to the famous one of the same name
the first time it played, because the only gate was a missing picture, which every new row has;
it is now repointed only when its own channel is dead (the page fails, or its title names
nobody), and otherwise just gets its own picture. A resolved credit is the row stored under the
channel's id, not whatever row has the name, which could be the placeholder itself, a local
artist or another channel; a placeholder of that name is folded into the channel once credited.

Feat. clauses are read as runs of names, with names the song already credits taken out first
(by artistKey), and the rest searched whole before it is split, so "Tyler, The Creator", "Earth,
Wind & Fire" and "Florence and the Machine" no longer add their pieces as artists. All writes for
a song now happen after the searching, in one transaction that gives up if the song's credits
changed meanwhile, under a lock shared with every artist merge, so a playback enrich, a rescan and
the refresher can no longer interleave and resurrect duplicates. The album backfill's unlink ran
asynchronously and could wipe the credits written after it; it is in the same transaction now.
Per-song duplicates compare by artistKey, featured credits append after the last position, and a
rename is written onto the stored row rather than over it, so a bookmark is not lost.

